{"timestamp": 1778546128.129326, "duration_sec": 2.4568605422973633, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "So they really put the dler into bun, I guess you could say. I was going to say, why did they call it the bun bundler and not just the bun dler? I know. Well, missed opportunity. But this is just announced a few days back, the bun fast native bundler. It's in beta. You now have a bun build command."], [0, "And I guess as with all things with Bunn, it's making news because their benchmarks show it to be very, very fast. Now, I guess buyer beware. We've had Bunn's benchmarks called into question by other people in the community, such as Ryan Dahl from Dino."], [0, "who just says, just check the numbers, guys. Got to look at those numbers again. And didn't say much else. So who knows? Synthetic benchmarks are what they are. But in their announcement post, Jared Sumner writes that, Bun, we have a benchmark comparing 10 copies of 3.js from scratch with source maps and minification. Webpack 5 does this in 38 seconds."], [0, "roll up does it in 32 parcel 2 in 26 seconds rspac four and a half es build pretty sticking fast guys 0.3 seconds but bun 0.17 seconds so this is a very fast thing what do you guys think fast enough to give it a shot don't care because es build's good enough"], [0, "I can think of a lot of things to do with that 0.13. You can do a lot of editor configs with that time. Just think about that. Every time you run it, you're saving yourself 0.13 seconds. I guess I feel like I don't have the, I don't really understand Bun's pitch. Like, why do I need to switch to a new run time?"], [0, "That is my question with this because I know nothing about it. Is this building it for Bun's runtime and then I can run it there? I guess it's just a generic because it's building client-side apps. I guess I answered my own question there. Yeah, I wouldn't imagine you need to use Bun with this because it's going to end up with a client-side compile thing. I mean, if we were still using Webpack, yeah, that's a huge difference. That's great. But I'm not. I'm using ES build. Yeah."], [0, "There you have it. I think there's other things they say is cool about it, but I didn't read the entire blog post. So let's go to the next piece of news because I'm not using Bunn, so I was just like, ah, Bunn Bundler, it's fast. Interesting. Let's chat. This one I did read more of, and I'm actually probably more excited about, but that's Baseline. Have you guys seen Mozilla's Baseline now? This is a new feature on MDN."], [0, "So this is like when you go to MDN and you see a particular feature such as CSS grid, the grid property. And then you think to yourself, can I use? And then you hop over to can I use and you can see the browser support. Well, they're going to build this right into the docs on Mozilla Developer Network now."], [0, "This baseline feature where every page will have, if it's baseline supported, a nice little label and a callout right there saying like this is a baseline feature of the web. And they define that as being it's supported across the most recent two versions of Firefox, Chrome, Edge, and Safari."], [0, "So for me, this is like something I always do. It's like save a step, right? So I look at a thing like, cool, I want to use this push notification API. And then I think to myself, can I actually use it or not? And I usually hop over and check the compatibility tables and all that. But for things that are broadly supported, they're just going to throw a label on it right there and save you a little time, maybe 0.3 seconds even before you have to go check for yourself. Don't they already have browser compat on MDN?"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["web development", "javascript", "build tools", "bundling", "performance benchmarks", "bun", "esbuild", "webpack", "runtime", "browser support", "mdn", "css", "web standards", "compatibility tables", "frontend development"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778546466.0178967, "duration_sec": 2.424152135848999, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Was on Friar Street."], [1, "Yep, yep, yep."], [0, "Right down from the Goosens."], [1, "Yep. Yeah, right down."], [0, "Yeah."], [1, "And, um, that, that was an honor, man, to be able to train in that gym. That was incredible. It was incredible."], [0, "Oh yeah, yeah, I used to be there. Yeah, yeah, man. Yeah, those are some great times because, I mean, I connect with Benny where\u2014 because when I was in Bridgeport, my instructor Manny Malice went to California and started training with Benny. Oh wow, they're early on. So he put that on the map about coming to the mecca and training with Benny."], [1, "It was the mecca for kickboxing, especially in the '90s. That was the mecca. You had to go to the Jet Center."], [0, "Everyone talked about the Jet Center. Yeah, man, that was\u2014 So we were."], [1, "Always wondering, like, what is the style? What's the best style? And then the UFC came along and I'm like, oh my God, they did it. They did it. They figured it out. They put it all together. And for a while it was jiu-jitsu because nobody understood jiu-jitsu and Royce Gracie was just running shit."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["martial arts", "kickboxing", "mixed martial arts", "ufc", "jiu jitsu", "training", "gym", "fighting styles", "combat sports", "martial arts history", "california", "bridgeport", "sports culture", "nineties"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778546695.5835392, "duration_sec": 1.6383893489837646, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "You get to go to all of these different places. And at the very least, the conference is going to cover a hotel for one or two, maybe three nights. And they're going to give you a flight there and back. And it's certainly still you have to pay out of pocket. Like last conference we went to, I was just like."], [0, "Man, like I spent hundreds of dollars on taxis to get to the airport and I'm buying a $12 crappy bagel at the airport. And it's definitely still hundreds of dollars out of your own pocket, especially if you don't have a company that's like comping any of that stuff. But the travel for free is pretty sweet. Sometimes these conferences will give you an honorarium or they'll pay you to do that. You have to be a little bit careful around..."], [0, "if you are getting paid because then you are dealing with visas and everything like that if you're crossing borders. But they can sometimes pay you. A lot of people have very strong opinions about if you should get paid or not to speak. Do you have any thoughts on that, Scott? I think it depends on..."], [0, "who you're speaking for and where you're speaking. Like, you know, there's some conferences where I really like the organization. I really like the people running it. And for me, it's like not like a corporation that is making a ton of money off of this conference, but maybe it's just like good people putting this thing on or it's a technology that isn't flush with cash that you could"], [0, "essentially feel like you're you're taking advantage of them but if some if a friend or a company or something they want me to speak and it's something that i really am passionate about i'll do it for free with the travel as long as everything works out um because it does you know and the whole doing it for exposure argument is kind of annoying but it is real exposure it's not fake exposure"], [0, "You know, like there is like a real benefit to getting that exposure. So I'm happy to do it for the exposure, given the right circumstance. But if you want me to, you know, speak at this corporate event or something like that, I think I would like to get paid if the company has the ability to pay you. I think that is, yeah."], [0, "That's generally how I feel it out. Is that like, A, is it going to be enjoyable for me? Like, I love going to conferences. Sometimes I'm just like, I don't care. I'll just do that. Other times it's just like, this is more of like an arrangement. You want me to come speak to the conference. I hate saying this, but like, I'm kind of a big draw. We have a big podcast. We can talk about it. Kind of a big draw. Kind of a big draw, yeah."], [0, "I kind of I hate saying that because it sounds very cocky, but the reason why they they try to get people that have lots of Twitter followers and a big podcast to speak at the thing is because we will talk about it and people will buy tickets to it. Right. And right. People are buying the ticket because you're there, not just because. Yeah. Yeah. At some point. Yeah. So there's that. I'm kind of in and out on whether sometimes I get paid money."], [0, "a couple thousand bucks to go speak. And sometimes I lose a couple hundred bucks off of it. So it really depends. And of course, I'm coming from a place of privilege where I can say that. But yeah, I think that there is also major benefits aside from money to speaking at conferences, like I just said."], [0, "And then the other thing is you can also like you can sometimes tack on like a training the day before or after the conference. And in-person workshops pay much better than speaking does. It's a lot more work because you're doing a whole day workshop. But that is a pretty common thing these days with conferences. At the very least, they'll say, hey, like..."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["conferences", "public speaking", "travel", "event planning", "compensation", "honorarium", "visas", "corporate events", "networking", "professional development", "marketing", "social media", "podcasting", "workshops", "training"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
