r volumes of delinquent loans , Trepp reported. Although the overall delinquency rate ended 2025 at 7.30%, office remained the most stressed sector, peaking at an all-time high of 11.76% before closing the year ..."], [2, "How To Find Distressed Properties - Local Foreclosures For Sale\nInstant Access: Search Foreclosures , Short Sales, Tax Liens, Auctions , and More. Find The Right Home For You. Discover The Latest Foreclosure Listings In Your Area."], [3, "Reo Bank Owned Homes For Sale - Properties For Sale\nFind HUD Foreclosure Fortunes - Access Our Database Of Foreclosures , Short Sales & More. Search Homes & Connect With a Real Estate Agent To Purchase the Home of Your Dreams."], [4, "REO, Pre-Foreclosed, HUD, FSBO - Distressed Real Estate\nCompare Search for Foreclosed Real Estate . Latest Bank Foreclosed Listings. Get access to thousands of local bank foreclosure listings, short sales, & sheriff sales."]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["health and safety committee", "darrell watson", "kevin flynn", "serena gonzales-gutierrez", "sarah parady", "amanda sandoval", "amanda sawyer", "jamie torres", "anne wallace", "council resolution 26-0340", "council resolution 26-0341", "neon forensics llc", "leon kelly", "emergent forensic pathology services", "weecycle", "healthy food for denver\u2019s kids initiative", "denver", "kbra", "trepp", "hud"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778548370.3799496, "duration_sec": 2.577505111694336, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "If you do Perl, where one person typically writes a script, runs it for a day or two and then throws it away, I mean, maybe simplifying a bit, then readability is irrelevant because you don't ever read it again except when you write it."], [0, "It's been described as a write-only language. Yes, exactly. So you have to know these constraints or these environment conditions, and then you have to define your language appropriately. There are interesting things you can do. For example, in projectional editors, the same language can have different notations."], [0, "And you can, for example, in our case, you can project a state machine as a table, as text, and very soon also as graphics. And then different stakeholders can make different decisions and edit the same thing in different ways. And for example, the textual notation is much more writable, but the tabular notation for certain kinds of things is much more readable because it gives you a grid of events and states and so you can easily do completeness checks."], [0, "So some kinds of tools let you postpone that decision because you can change it afterwards. But that's a kind of special case. So in your experience, how important is in practice the ability to involve the domain expert? Do you actually see domain experts who are not programmers writing code or more reading domain-specific language code or they just expect someone else, a programmer, to do it?"], [0, "Let's put it this way. Traditionally, DSLs have been used in technical domains, where in some cases the domain experts were the programmers. Like, for example, if I have an architecture DSL for JEE applications, then it's probably going to be used, the language is going to be used by the same person who writes the JEE code."], [0, "So it's not really an involvement of actual domain experts. But this is changing. For example, we've built a language for configuring refrigerator control cooling algorithms."], [0, "And there, the thermodynamics person, the physicist that dealt with these control algorithms or cooling algorithms was actually involved in writing these programs and also in designing the language. Also in this financial case, this guy Herman, who would retire, he was involved in defining the right abstractions that go into the language. And then the users were actual insurance, I don't know how you call them, clerks is probably the wrong word, people who create and maintain insurance plans."], [0, "And we're actually now building yet another language in the insurance domain that has the same kind of users. So yes, these kinds of domain experts, non-programmers, they are involved. And sure, maybe in the beginning they need some help, so they start reading some of these programs and more programmer types write them. But, you know, many of these people will start recognizing patterns and getting what you do."], [0, "And they like to be in charge. They like to be able to change insurance plans without calling up the programmer and asking them whether they kindly have time to help them. And that's a strong motivation to learn these DSLs. And if you build them involving the stakeholders in the first place, you can build languages that will be used by non-programmers. And I've seen it and I've done it. It's not necessarily the simplest and default case, but they do exist."], [0, "And it's hugely intellectually satisfying when you get to the point where you can have a non-developer go and write. It means I think you have succeeded in your DSL design. Yes. And I mean, it's ridiculous. I mean, the way we develop software these days is completely ridiculous. That domain people take Word or Excel, write down unchecked, imprecise, inconsistent things. And that's not because they're idiots. That's because they don't have a tool that checks for the consistency, right?"]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["domain specific languages", "language design", "software engineering", "projectional editors", "state machines", "code readability", "non programmers", "domain experts", "modeling tools", "insurance systems", "configuration languages", "stakeholder collaboration", "software tools", "consistency checking", "control algorithms"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
