{"timestamp": 1778549527.0130994, "duration_sec": 2.2989509105682373, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah, what a humbling thing. Yeah, yeah, very humbling."], [1, "Yeah, and what's really humbling is how quickly someone could do it to you when you don't know what you're doing. Like, that was shocking to me because I had all this martial arts experience, and I first started training, I was like, what's someone gonna do to me? I wrestled in high school, I'm strong, I'm fast, I know how to fight. Yeah, I just got manhandled over and over and over again. Oh yeah, it's ridiculous."], [0, "Yeah, see that? But kudos because you A lot of people, because of, if you got an egotistical thing going and you get that your little, I don't know, your comfort 'cause you got your black belt and all that kind of stuff, that means jack nothing. Everybody I know who continues and really to learn real fighting knows when you had a boxer beat the hell out you and you go, oh wait, there's a lot of this stuff I gotta toss out the window. 'Cause I mean, I never forget, times where, you know, like a wrestler gets to me or a boxer like pieces me up like early on. I'm like, no, I got to learn this. Oh yeah."], [1, "Yeah. This episode is brought to you by SimpliSafe. You shouldn't have to stress about keeping your home and family safe from intruders on top of everything else going on right now. So don't. Take a load off with SimpliSafe. It's been named one of the best home security systems by U.S. News and World Report 5 years in a row. They're always striving to do better and unlike unlike traditional security systems, SimpliSafe takes action before the worst can happen. AI-powered cameras and live professional monitoring agents keep an eye on your property. If they detect anything weird or suspicious, they can dispatch police, turn on spotlights, and even talk to intruders to help deter them. With SimpliSafe, you can be confident that your home and loved ones are in good hands, so you can focus on other things in your life. Like landing your dream job, date nights with your partner, bulking up at the gym, or planning the ultimate ice fishing trip with the guys. It's time to stop worrying and focus on living your life. With SimpliSafe, get 50% off your new SimpliSafe system with professional monitoring and your first month free at simplisafe.com/rogan. That's 50% off with your first month free at SimpliSafe simplysafe.com/rogan. There's no safe like SimpliSafe. This episode is brought to you by ThreatLocker. Here's the truth: reacting to cyber attacks isn't enough. You need control from the start. That's where ThreatLocker comes in. With zero trust, you only allow what you need, blocking everything else by default. You're in control what runs when, where, and how. Ransomware, zero-day exploits, block them before they can execute. And now with AI now increasing the speed and volume of attacks, a fast response isn't fast enough. Visit threatlocker.com/JRE to learn more. I went through several of those. I went through one of them in high school because I have a, had a friend in high school that was a wrestler and I I didn't think anything of wrestling. I'm like, that's not even a martial art. And then we wrestled on the grass one day and he just took me down at will. Yeah, I was like, this is ridiculous. Yeah, he was pinning me down. I couldn't, I couldn't move. I couldn't get up. I'm like, this is stunning, right? I thought I'm strong. Yeah, I thought I could move. I thought I'll be able to get out of the way. No, not\u2014 I had no chance. Yeah, he wasn't even a great wrestler. He was just a decent wrestler. Yeah, he just humbled me over and over and over again. So then I started wrestling. And then when I got into taekwondo, I thought, I'm really good at taekwondo. I was competing on a national level. I won the state championships 4 years in a row. I was fucking people up. And then I remember the first time I boxed with a really good amateur boxer. I was like, oh Lord. And this kid was like 18 years old. And I was at the gym. And he went on\u2014 his kid, his name is Dana Rosenblatt. He went on to become New England middleweight champion. Vinnie Pazienza. He beat Howard Davis Jr. as a professional. He was a really good boxer."], [0, "Well, yeah, he had to be."], [1, "But he was kickboxing at the time, and I was gonna get into kickboxing, and so I was sparring with him. But when I was boxing with him, I was just getting lit up. I was like, oh. And then also, when we were kickboxing, the moment he got close to me, I was in trouble, right? I was like, oh no. Like, taekwondo had too many flaws. Exactly. The hand techniques So I had gone through that, and so then I thought, okay, well now I understand kickboxing. Then I met a dude who went to Thailand a bunch of times and was training Muay Thai and fighting over there, and then I started learning leg kicks. I'm like, well, oh good lord, now all they have to do is kick my legs? I didn't even think of that. Yeah, and then I started really paying attention to WKA fights, the old Dennis Alexio days, Don the Dragon Wilson. Leg kicks, leg kicks are everything. Oh my goodness. Yeah, and then I'm like, okay, well now I got a solid foundation, I understand how to fight, and And then I started getting jiu-jitsu. Like, oh no, back to square one. I'm getting raped. I was just getting mauled on the mats. But I'd been through that so many times and restarted so many times. I was like, well, it's time to learn this now."], [0, "Yeah, that's what I'm saying is everything has something to teach you. And even though there's that, there's a martial art, there's a fantasy world, which is, I look at it as hilarious. You know, there's this, you know, I don't know, there's sometimes, I would say it like this, like with martial arts, it's the Dunning-Kruger effect in the largest way possible because everybody out there has an opinion of martial arts, though very few people really know what it is. You know, they want to look at, you know, the movies and everything, and they really want to believe that. They want to believe that this guy who, you know, kicks in the air and all that kind of stuff will be able to beat a champion. And in a way, hey, I benefit from that to some degree because they, you know, they think that about me. But, you know, even though I'm comfortable fighting and I love to\u2014 I mean, I just love fighting against anybody, you know, but\u2014 But you've had actual competition."], [1, "Experience, like a lot of competition experience."], [0, "Yeah, but my best experience is is with like, I got a chance to train against champions at their place, you know, when they're at their best. And it's not an ego thing. It's just like, I love to be able to test myself. And I mean, because I'm my biggest competition. And so that whole thing about just what the bow means to me is like, thank you for\u2014 making me better, right, by providing me an obstacle. And the higher the\u2014 you know, the better the person, the better I can become. 100%."], [1, "And so I loved it."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["martial arts", "fighting", "sparring", "wrestling", "boxing", "kickboxing", "muay thai", "jiu jitsu", "training", "competition", "humility", "ego", "self improvement", "dunning kruger", "combat sports"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778550762.0976458, "duration_sec": 3.2477715015411377, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Untertitelung. BR 2018"], [0, "Herzlich willkommen zum OT3D-Werkstatt-Podcast, deinem Insider-Podcast. Wenn es um die digitale Revolution in der Orthop\u00e4die-Werkstatt geht, entdecke, wie innovative Technologien wie zum Beispiel der 3D-Druck deine Arbeitsprozesse im Sanit\u00e4tshaus oder der Orthop\u00e4die-Werkstatt ver\u00e4ndern und welche Chancen sich daraus f\u00fcr dich ergeben. In jeder Episode bieten wir praxisnahe Einblicke, Experteninterviews und wertvolle Tipps, um dein Unternehmen auf die n\u00e4chste digitale Stufe zu bringen,"], [0, "Egal, ob du Gesch\u00e4ftsf\u00fchrer, Orthop\u00e4die-Techniker oder technikbegeistert bist, hier erf\u00e4hrst du alles, was du \u00fcber die Zukunft der Orthop\u00e4die-Technik wissen musst."], [0, "Hallo und herzlich willkommen in der OT3D-Werkstatt, deinem Podcast f\u00fcr digitale Prozesse und Innovation in deiner OT-Werkstatt. Ich bin Dominik Heinz und heute gibt es eine proaktive These, die so proaktiv ist, dass mich neulich jemand angesprochen hatte in einer wunderbaren, tollen Diskussion und die Aussage getroffen hatte. 3D-Druck in der Orthop\u00e4die ist total absurd. Hm."], [0, "Klingt erstmal komplett falsch, aber mag vielleicht doch sein. Aber heute schauen wir doch mal genauer hin, weil das Ganze hat mich ein bisschen bewegt im Kopf. Ich habe ein paar Tage dar\u00fcber nachgedacht. Und ja, 3D-Druck in der Orthop\u00e4die-Technik ist absurd, wenn du ihn falsch angehst. So habe ich es ja zumindest f\u00fcr mich mal so ein bisschen definiert."], [0, "Und was ich damit genau meine, erkl\u00e4re ich euch jetzt nochmal ein bisschen tiefer. Aber bleibt auf jeden Fall dran und h\u00f6rt zu. Wenn euch die Folge generell gef\u00e4llt, bitte macht einen Daumen hoch bei eurem Podcast Player. Aber ich lege jetzt einfach mal los. In dieser Folge..."], [0, "zeige ich dir mal so ein bisschen die Missverst\u00e4ndnisse oder die gro\u00dfen Missverst\u00e4ndnisse, Fehlannahmen oder auch Irrwege rund um das Thema 3D-Druck, Einsatz in der Orthop\u00e4die-Technik. Und ich zeige euch mal, wie du aus Absurdit\u00e4ten echten Mehrwert machen kannst. Genau. Und da habe ich wieder mir ein paar Themen \u00fcberlegt, ein paar Fragen."], [0, "\u00dcberschriften habe ich mir hier auf den Zettel geschrieben. Und der erste Punkt, den ich hier habe, warum manche wirklich glauben, 3D-Druck sei Quatsch? Also warum denken denn manche Leute, 3D-Druck sei Quatsch? Naja, lass uns doch mal ehrlich sein. Viele, die den Satz sagen, 3D-Druck in der OT ist absurd, meinen eigentlich..."], [0, "Das ist doch nur Spielerei. Oder das kostet doch nur Geld. Oder das bringt uns in der Werkstatt keinen echten Vorteil. Oder damit kann ich doch keine echten Patientenversorgungen drucken. Und der letzte Punkt, den ich noch habe, das ist was f\u00fcr Konzerne, nicht f\u00fcr den Alltag. Also nicht f\u00fcr uns. Wir sind viel zu klein daf\u00fcr. Kurz mal nachgedacht. Und wei\u00dft du was?"], [0, "Wenn man den 3D-Druck einfach irgendwo hinstellt, ohne Integration, ohne Prozess, ohne Plan, dann stimmt es sogar von wegen, 3D-Druck ist dann wirklich absurd und ineffizient. Vollkommen richtig. Weil er hat halt einfach keine Wirkung. Er kann seine Wirkung, seine Vorteile nicht entfalten. Genau, zweiter Punkt, den ich mir aufgeschrieben hatte,"]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["3d printing", "orthopedics", "prosthetics", "medical devices", "digital transformation", "manufacturing", "workflow automation", "innovation", "technology adoption", "small business", "process integration", "patient care", "cost efficiency", "design engineering", "healthcare technology"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778550939.5268326, "duration_sec": 2.8483023643493652, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "The pipeline only forwards a completed user turn to the LLM when AssemblyEye's neural model is confident the speaker has finished. This is meaningfully different from VOD, voice activity detection, which triggers on silence, not on conversational intent."], [0, "ko api key dashboard daily ko openai api key cartesia api key play cartesia i step 1 install dependencies the pipecat i extras pull in the official service integrations the solero extra installs the solero vod model which we use for pre-filtering silence before sending audio to assembly ai"], [0, "create a env file step 2 create a daily coroom daily corooms are ephemeral you create them via api get a url and use that url to join here's a helper script run it keep that url you'll pass it to the bot in the next step"], [0, "Step 3. Build the bot. Here's the complete agent. We'll walk through each section after the full listing. Step 4. Run it. Open that URL in your browser, grant microphone access, and start talking."], [0, "The bot will respond in real time. Code walkthrough. The transport layer we disable daily's built-in transcription. Transcription underscore enabled equals false. Because we're routing audio to assembly AI instead. Celero VOD runs locally to filter out silence before it ever leaves the machine. This reduces latency and API cost."], [0, "The VOD underscore audio underscore pass through equals true flag is important. Even though VOD is gating what gets counted as speech, the raw audio still flows through to assembly AI. The STT model does its own analysis on that audio. VOD just keeps us from processing dead silence. Turn detection The three turn detection parameters work together. End underscore of underscore turn underscore confidence underscore threshold equals zero."], [0, "7. The model needs 70% confidence that the user has finished their turn before it signals turn end. Lower this if you want faster, but potentially premature, responses. min underscore end underscore of underscore turn underscore silence underscore when underscore confident equals 300. Even when confidence is high, wait at least 300 milliseconds of silence before firing."], [0, "prevents cutting off speakers who pause for emphasis. Max underscore turn underscore silence equals 1000. After one second of silence, force a turn end regardless of confidence. The safety valve. This is the part that actually matters for voice agent quality. Most latency problems in voice agents aren't the STT or LLM. They're bad turn detection that either interrupts the user too early or waits too long after they've finished."], [0, "Neural turn detection, which Assembly AI calls universal turn detection, differs from VOD-based approaches in that it's trained to understand conversational intent, not just audio energy. It handles cases like trailing off mid-sentence but clearly completing a thought or pausing to think before continuing."], [0, "The pipeline pipecat pipelines are lists of processors. Frames flow left to right, or down the list. Each processor handles the frame types it understands and passes everything else through. The ordering after the LLM matters. TTS comes before transport output, because the LLM emits text chunks that need to be synthesized before they can be played."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Technology"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["voice agents", "speech recognition", "turn taking", "voice activity detection", "conversation intent", "real time audio", "latency", "neural networks", "audio processing", "api integration", "bot development", "text to speech", "large language models", "pipeline architecture", "cost optimization"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778551407.0005212, "duration_sec": 3.3066985607147217, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "I actually never used it, but I guess the reason I put it here is it's not like solving the same problems, but it's just like the only project that I know that parsing, I'm not sure what it does internally, but deals with messages. It deals with the categorization."], [0, "of messages into errors and warnings so yeah it's the the only project that i know mostly like i put it here because of marketing because people could find it like there's beckon like what could i find else in google now i am kind of curious right because it seems a lot of people are using uh vs code when they're writing basically everything especially rust because it works pretty well rust analyzer now you chose neo vim as your main driver why choose neo vim over any other editor"], [0, "Yeah, the reason is because it's just hard to click with new Veeam. It's very addictive. It's just, yeah, it's hard to switch between various development environments and just new Veeam or Veeam. Actually, initially I started with Veeam. It allows a lot of customization that I want. And I just cannot have this kind of customization in other things."], [0, "Unfortunately, or I can have them, but I need to write them into like very good take a lot of time. Does Neo Vim also integrate with rust analyzer?"], [0, "yeah it is but it is hard to use rust analyzer in large projects because or like middle-sized projects because yeah bugs it can suddenly stop finding your errors warnings things like that happening it can be slow so yeah that's why i use cargo limits to get to the warnings and errors quickly and i actually use"], [0, "separate temporary directories for rust analyzer and everything else like cargo limit clippy and the things that produce the binaries because i don't know how it's working right now but the last time i tried to use it in the same directory i was running into a locking all the time like rust analyzer just locks the building process all the time that was a problem for me it locks the building process is that what you're saying"], [0, "Yeah, probably because of some workaround that they might cargo check in certain cases. And that's where I cannot run cargo builds. I need to wait until the cargo check will finish in the Rust analyzing background somewhere. I see."], [0, "Yeah, I think this is a pretty interesting project, right? I'm surprised that we don't have something like this already in the compiler because it is super helpful, right? You can just, yeah, as you're building, you can just run it, see if there's something that stops your app from running and go straight to it. And your integration with NeoVim,"], [0, "It opens up the line automatically, right? And I think that you already have a way that other editors can use similar style, right? As long as they can take in that JSON and react to it. Isn't that what I think I've seen? Because you have like a separate section about how to integrate with other editors, but it's very generic saying you have to be able to read this JSON."], [0, "Yeah, it's possible to integrate with other editors. If you really want, you can use gedit or whatever application that has some API to work with. You can integrate if you really want. Do you have any more features coming up anytime soon? What's your next kind of couple of things that you really want to work out? Because you do have a couple of features that... Actually, sorry, you have about, what, eight issues in CargoLimit that are waiting to be worked on for feature-wise."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["rust", "neovim", "text editors", "vs code", "rust analyzer", "compiler errors", "warnings", "message parsing", "build tools", "cargo", "clippy", "developer workflow", "customization", "editor integration", "json output"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
