nt wrestler. Yeah, he just humbled me over and over and over again. So then I started wrestling. And then when I got into taekwondo, I thought, I'm really good at taekwondo. I was competing on a national level. I won the state championships 4 years in a row. I was fucking people up. And then I remember the first time I boxed with a really good amateur boxer. I was like, oh Lord. And this kid was like 18 years old. And I was at the gym. And he went on\u2014 his kid, his name is Dana Rosenblatt. He went on to become New England middleweight champion. Vinnie Pazienza. He beat Howard Davis Jr. as a professional. He was a really good boxer."], [0, "Well, yeah, he had to be."], [1, "But he was kickboxing at the time, and I was gonna get into kickboxing, and so I was sparring with him. But when I was boxing with him, I was just getting lit up. I was like, oh. And then also, when we were kickboxing, the moment he got close to me, I was in trouble, right? I was like, oh no. Like, taekwondo had too many flaws. Exactly. The hand techniques So I had gone through that, and so then I thought, okay, well now I understand kickboxing. Then I met a dude who went to Thailand a bunch of times and was training Muay Thai and fighting over there, and then I started learning leg kicks. I'm like, well, oh good lord, now all they have to do is kick my legs? I didn't even think of that. Yeah, and then I started really paying attention to WKA fights, the old Dennis Alexio days, Don the Dragon Wilson. Leg kicks, leg kicks are everything. Oh my goodness. Yeah, and then I'm like, okay, well now I got a solid foundation, I understand how to fight, and And then I started getting jiu-jitsu. Like, oh no, back to square one. I'm getting raped. I was just getting mauled on the mats. But I'd been through that so many times and restarted so many times. I was like, well, it's time to learn this now."], [0, "Yeah, that's what I'm saying is everything has something to teach you. And even though there's that, there's a martial art, there's a fantasy world, which is, I look at it as hilarious. You know, there's this, you know, I don't know, there's sometimes, I would say it like this, like with martial arts, it's the Dunning-Kruger effect in the largest way possible because everybody out there has an opinion of martial arts, though very few people really know what it is. You know, they want to look at, you know, the movies and everything, and they really want to believe that. They want to believe that this guy who, you know, kicks in the air and all that kind of stuff will be able to beat a champion. And in a way, hey, I benefit from that to some degree because they, you know, they think that about me. But, you know, even though I'm comfortable fighting and I love to\u2014 I mean, I just love fighting against anybody, you know, but\u2014 But you've had actual competition."], [1, "Experience, like a lot of competition experience."], [0, "Yeah, but my best experience is is with like, I got a chance to train against champions at their place, you know, when they're at their best. And it's not an ego thing. It's just like, I love to be able to test myself. And I mean, because I'm my biggest competition. And so that whole thing about just what the bow means to me is like, thank you for\u2014 making me better, right, by providing me an obstacle. And the higher the\u2014 you know, the better the person, the better I can become. 100%."], [1, "And so I loved it."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["martial arts", "combat sports", "wrestling", "boxing", "kickboxing", "muay thai", "jiu jitsu", "sparring", "self defense", "training", "competition", "humility", "ego", "skill development", "dunning kruger"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778559434.191017, "duration_sec": 2.7812914848327637, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "So there's a lot to this. There's a lot to see here. We're accustomed because of NCIS and CSI, TV shows, that kind of thing, Rob, to thinking things get sold quickly. And typically, they do get sold quickly in this era. But plainly, the people who have done this took great pains to cover who they were and what they were doing."], [0, "We've got a couple minutes left. We're talking with Private Detective Nils Gravelius about the state of the Nancy Guthrie investigation, which is ongoing but has been bumped from the headlines. There were a lot of people who were concerned about Savannah Guthrie getting star treatment or extra attention with the FBI when there are a lot of other missing people or people disappeared under strange circumstances that don't get the FBI. Was there some sort of favoritism in play here?"], [0, "I don't think that there is favoritism at play. I mean, if somebody wants to go that angle, if you look at Savannah Guthrie and Nancy Guthrie, their public media presence, et cetera, has been somewhat unfriendly to Donald Trump, that sort of thing. I don't think that Donald Trump would allow that to color what he tells Patel to do in this. Any missing woman, any missing vulnerable person"], [0, "The family should expect law enforcement action. Things don't always come out the way that they appear. There are times when law enforcement does drop the ball, but I don't think that they're getting special treatment. I think the special treatment that we're seeing, Rob, is in the media, not in law enforcement."], [0, "Is that the wall-to-wall coverage that went on for about three weeks? Is that what you're referring to? That's what I'm referring to. I mean, it would be like the O.J. Simpson case. There was practically a base camp set up opposite criminal courts building in Los Angeles for two and a half years. Well, they covered that. Well, people get killed rather frequently in Los Angeles. No other murder trial was covered to the extent that the Simpson case was."], [0, "Nils, let's talk about the video evidence. They had to go back, and they didn't immediately let on or didn't know they had this video evidence in the cloud. They found it. Was there anything particular in the video that would help the investigation? I know the guy had it looked like a ski mask on, but what other sorts of clues were there? Well, the type of gun that he had, which appeared to be a SIG maybe or maybe a Smith & Wesson M&P, I'm not entirely sure. Yeah."], [0, "He appeared to have a tattoo that was partially visible in the video. He was very well equipped for every single thing other than the camera. Remember, he hung a weed up in front of the camera to obscure the lens and then ultimately removed the camera from the house and took the camera with him when he took Nancy Guthrie. Finally, Nils, are you confident that this crime will be solved or do you think this is the sort of crime that may linger for years?"], [0, "It will eventually be solved. I'm fairly confident on that. I've compared it a couple of times to the JonBenet Ramsey case, where it was misidentified as a kidnapping at the start, and they had already contaminated the crime scene by the time they realized it was a murder, and that's the principal reason they'll never bring charges against the primary suspect in that case."], [0, "Nils, I know your book is coming out sometime soon, The Last Lawman. Tell us how we can get more information and how we can be in touch with you. Well, I'm at DetectiveNils on X, formerly Twitter. My book is out in June, but you can order it today on Amazon. It is titled The Last Lawman, True Stories of a Private Detective, and it is my true-to-life memoir, 30 Years of Running and Gunning on the Streets of Los Angeles, Robb."], [0, "I can't wait to read it. Nils Gravelius, thanks for spending some time with us on The Schilling Show today. My sincere pleasure. You have a lovely day in Virginia, Rob. Thank you so much, and we will. All right, let's go to the break. Coming back on the other side, these things are anxiety-causing. Dr. Dan Elash, life relationship and business coach, will be joining us, mitigating stress and anxiety. I'm going to be listening very carefully. Stay with The Schilling Show. We'll get into this after news and weather at the bottom of the hour."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["crime investigation", "missing persons", "law enforcement", "private detective", "fbi", "media coverage", "video evidence", "forensic evidence", "crime scene", "kidnapping", "murder trial", "true crime", "criminal psychology", "firearms", "anxiety and stress"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
