{"timestamp": 1778566139.7714374, "duration_sec": 2.957545518875122, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "A mob boss?"], [1, "Yeah, he was a dangerous, dangerous guy. He was the guy that that movie that Leonardo DiCaprio starred in with Jack Nicholson. What was that movie, Jamie? Remember that movie that was based on Whitey Bulger? The Departed? Yeah, yeah, yeah. Okay, that was based on Whitey Bulger."], [0, "He was a gangster?"], [1, "Oh, yeah. Terrifying gangster."], [0, "Yeah. What was his demise?"], [1, "He was actually a fucking FBI informant. Not only was he a gangster, he was working with the FBI, and they were letting him get away with shit because he was throwing other people under the bus."], [0, "I think there's a different\u2014 I mean\u2014."], [1, "They wound up catching him in Santa Monica. Well, I've found that\u2014 Hunting Whitey. About that Whitey Bulger won the Mass Millions lottery about that time. Yeah. Yeah, he won the fucking lottery, man. So this is what it says here. What does it say? I think it was more of a scheme than they actually won, but it's always taking money. It's probably a way to like launder money."], [0, "Yeah."], [1, "Oh, 100%. It's a way to launder money. So the way it would work was like, say if you lived in the community and you won the lottery ticket, maybe they would give you money for your lottery ticket, then he would get it, and that way it would show that this is where he got his income from. Like, these guys would all own businesses, right? But the reason why they would own businesses is so they could show why they drive a Cadillac, why they have a mansion, why they have this\u2014 because they have legitimate businesses. But really, these businesses were scams."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["organized crime", "mob", "gangsters", "mafia", "fbi", "informants", "corruption", "crime", "money laundering", "lottery", "scams", "law enforcement", "true crime", "film", "biography"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778566226.6047304, "duration_sec": 2.0320897102355957, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Doesn't that become, I don't want to be triggering or anything, but doesn't that."], [1, "Become destructive after a while?"], [0, "100%. And do you\u2014 have you noticed, especially\u2014 I'll put it like this\u2014 especially with comedians with podcasts, it used to be a time where, like you say, a person would go on a podcast because it was interesting, they told funny stories, right, or they was good at their craft or whatever. But now it feels like all these platforms\u2014 and I don't know, that's just in my community when I say that, Black people\u2014 it's like the only way I can find myself interested if I talk shit about people, right? And motherfuckers are going away from being funny when you get interviewed. Like, every fucking podcast I turn on now, it's somebody\u2014 I'm exposed this person, I'm gonna tell this about what you didn't know. And the one thing they're not doing, especially as a stand-up comedian, motherfuckers don't give a fuck about being funny no more. Are those days over?"], [1, "No, no, no, no. Those people that do that are almost always not very talented. Almost always. The only exception\u2014 so who they fooling? The only exception to that is Cat. And I think what Cat was doing was different because what Cat was doing was exposing what he thought was snakes and liars."], [0, "Okay, it's a different thing. Okay, okay, okay, okay, this is my question. Okay, okay, this is my question. Okay, this is my question."], [1, "People can take it."], [0, "Okay, okay, I want to say this. This is what I'm saying, Joe. What are you saying? I'm coming from this place. I'm like this, no disrespect or whatever to Cat, but Who asked."], [1, "You? Shannon Sharpe did when he did that podcast."], [0, "Shannon Sharpe might have asked one question."], [1, "But Shannon Sharpe likes that. Like that in his relationship, he likes a lot of that. His, his people, they find questions. He's got a sheet of paper."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["comedy", "stand up", "podcasts", "interviewing", "entertainment", "celebrity gossip", "conflict", "criticism", "exposure", "talent", "humor", "media culture", "social media", "accountability", "black culture"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778566368.163976, "duration_sec": 2.0178616046905518, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Pues ojal\u00e1 sepamos... ...sepamos si fue... ...con premeditaci\u00f3n... ...porque... ...\u00bfc\u00f3mo iba a saber el vigilante? \u00bfNo? Que iba a ir..."], [0, "Entonces, ah\u00ed la premeditaci\u00f3n est\u00e1 medio extra\u00f1a, \u00bfno? Pero bueno, vamos a esperar m\u00e1s informaci\u00f3n. M\u00e9xico busca eliminar aranceles en sectores clave durante la negociaci\u00f3n del tratado con Canad\u00e1 y con los Estados Unidos, el T-MEC. Y de esto le da cuenta Sebasti\u00e1n Garc\u00eda. Adelante, Sebasti\u00e1n."], [0, "La presidenta Claudia Sheinbaum sostuvo una reuni\u00f3n con Jamison Greer, titular de la oficina del representante comercial de Estados Unidos, como parte de la segunda ronda de negociaciones rumbo a la revisi\u00f3n de T-MEC. El encuentro se realiza en Palacio Nacional con el objetivo de avanzar en acuerdos bilaterales estrat\u00e9gicos."], [0, "Uno de los principales temas en la agenda fue la eliminaci\u00f3n o revisi\u00f3n de aranceles en sectores clave como el acero, el aluminio y la industria automotriz, los cuales han sido afectados por tarifas impuestas en administraciones anteriores. La mandataria subray\u00f3 que M\u00e9xico busca alcanzar acuerdos previos que permitan aliviar tensiones comerciales antes de la revisi\u00f3n formal del tratado. Adem\u00e1s, ambas partes abordaron el fortalecimiento de las reglas de origen,"], [0, "un punto impulsado por Estados Unidos para evitar que productos de terceros pa\u00edses ingresan a su mercado a trav\u00e9s de M\u00e9xico sin suficiente valor agregado. El gobierno mexicano coincide en reforzar estos mecanismos para favorecer la producci\u00f3n nacional. Las negociaciones estuvieron encabezadas por el secretario de Econom\u00eda, Marcelo Ebrard,"], [0, "junto a un equipo t\u00e9cnico especializado que incluy\u00f3 a funcionarios en materia econ\u00f3mica, agr\u00edcola y financiera. Esta segunda mesa de trabajo de continuidad a los encuentros previos realizados en Washington y busca mantener vigente el tratado, pero con ajustes que beneficien a la industria mexicana. Para Noticias Milet, Sebasti\u00e1n Garc\u00eda."], [0, "Bueno, pues ah\u00ed est\u00e1 Sebasti\u00e1n Garc\u00eda, hayan seis narcolaboratorios en Chihuahua, un fiscal destaca una magnitud extraordinaria, hablan como de 800 metros de laboratorio, con qu\u00edmicos, con todas las cocinas que usan para hacer estos productos. Julieta Mendoza le tiene todo el detalle. A ver, Julieta, cu\u00e9ntanos."], [0, "El fiscal de Chihuahua, C\u00e9sar Jauregui Moreno, revel\u00f3 que el operativo en el que participaron autoridades estatales, federales y personal vinculado a la Embajada de Estados Unidos permiti\u00f3 localizar y destruir seis narcolaboratorios clandestinos en el municipio de Morelos, al sur del estado."], [0, "De acuerdo con el funcionario, tras m\u00e1s de tres meses de investigaci\u00f3n conjunta entre la Agencia Estatal de Investigaci\u00f3n y la Secretar\u00eda de la Defensa Nacional, Jauregui calific\u00f3 el hallazgo como uno de los m\u00e1s grandes en el pa\u00eds debido a la infraestructura y volumen de insumos encontrados. En los campamentos aseguraron decenas de contenedores con sustancias qu\u00edmicas, hornos, calderas, cilindros de gas y materiales como acetona."], [0, "lo que evidencia una operaci\u00f3n a gran escala. El caso fue turnado a la Fiscal\u00eda General de la Rep\u00fablica al tratarse de delitos federales, mientras se presume que la producci\u00f3n estaba vinculada a una organizaci\u00f3n criminal con presencia en Sinaloa. El operativo concluy\u00f3 con la destrucci\u00f3n de los laboratorios, sin embargo, horas despu\u00e9s ocurri\u00f3 el accidente en el que murieron cuatro elementos, dos mexicanos y dos estadounidenses, cuando el convoy en el que viajaban cay\u00f3 a un barranco cerca del municipio de Iguachochi,"]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["mexico politics", "trade agreement", "tariffs", "international trade", "united states", "canada", "manufacturing", "steel", "automotive industry", "drug trafficking", "clandestine laboratories", "law enforcement", "organized crime", "border security", "investigation"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778567075.67513, "duration_sec": 2.8147342205047607, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "because I wanted to spread the knowledge that I was gaining from the other people. And I spoke to a couple of directors at RSA at the time, and then they were like, yeah, you bring the meetup in-house and we'll let you host it, we'll let you have people in it. So it was awesome, right? So I was really motivated by all of that. But then I realized what I'm missing is I'm seeing how big companies run, how they operate, but let me see how the small companies run."], [0, "So I took the risk and I said, okay, let me just go join a startup, a product startup. So that was my journey into seeing how a product works. And from a startup level, there was only like five people at the startup and everyone was wearing different hats. Sure. Getting started with it. Learned a ton there, right? Constant innovation, constantly grinding. Great, great, great time there. What I was thinking to myself at that time was, okay, now I got the product startup perspective. What if..."], [0, "How does services or consulting work, right? Let me go experiment that. So I joined a services startup, which their motto was consulting. A couple guys, amazing, amazing dudes. Got to work with them, see how they negotiate contracts, bring in different contracts. One of the contracts was so interesting to me. I was working on an app for someone, that was his hobby."], [0, "this idea of a virtual bar. So he was mapping out all the bars in the cities that they go to and would give the ability for someone to purchase a seat in the virtual world, which was a fascinating idea. I was like, people pay for this stuff? It's like, yeah, this is cool stuff, right? So I got really motivated by that and then"], [0, "eventually decided, okay, I'm just going to start this journey on my own and see how things go. So that's fast forward to 2021. Sure. And I worked with a client. And at that point, I decided, okay, the project's going really well. I think I can pull the plug on my full-time job and took that leap and never looked back. Gotcha."], [0, "So you were kind of a weekend warrior at that point. Yes. You had a job. Yep. Nights and weekends. I was wondering because for a lot of people going into their own business, especially a services business like a consultancy, the question is, how do I get that flywheel going? Do I just quit my job and take the leap or do I weekend warrior it for a while? So did you have a plan from the start or was it just kind of like opportunistic? Yeah."], [0, "Yeah, I think I jumped into the opportunity. Maybe in hindsight, I probably jumped in too early. But again, I have no regrets, right? There's never a good time, man. Yeah, absolutely. Right? Yeah. There really isn't. You can't time that stuff. It's like the market. You can't time the entrance into a stock. Oh, yeah, for sure. I mean, you can, but it's hard. Right. It's basically impossible. Yeah. Just get in. Yeah, absolutely. Yeah. The best time is now. Yeah. Oh, yeah. That's why you've been doing that for three years? Yes. What's the hardest part?"], [0, "Hardest part is managing the different clients and keep the pipeline full all the time, right? So now wearing different hats, not only consulting, not only coding, not only mentoring. Selling. Selling. Yeah. Closing. Invoicing. Invoicing. Collecting. Selling, collecting money. Contracts are tough, too, because you want to scrutinize those contracts. Those contracts are obviously like..."], [0, "words of bond. So it's got to be clear. And you don't want your client relationship to go haywire because you did not word your contract well enough. There's always little details between each contract that changes. And there's a lot of details in that process. And finding the right people who actually write the check. Some companies, it's the CTO who does that. Some companies, it's not. The CTO still has to talk to the CFO or the senior engineer will have to go talk to someone else. So getting everyone on board. Do you have to spend a lot of time hunting down a check?"], [0, "What's that? Do you have to spend a lot of time hunting down this check? Once you've delivered your invoice, is there sometimes like, hey, you know, y'all owe us the money the invoice said to pay us? I've been fortunate so far. Okay. So, knock on wood. You'll hit it. You'll hit it eventually, especially larger the org, the less they care. What's your TTP? What's that mean? I'm making this up right now. Time to payment."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["entrepreneurship", "startups", "consulting", "freelance", "small business", "client management", "sales", "contracts", "invoicing", "cash flow", "business development", "career change", "product development", "networking", "risk taking"], "vectors