{"timestamp": 1778569767.9440253, "duration_sec": 2.041022777557373, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "This is one of the first things that your employer is very likely to see about you, this first page of Google. And if they can't find you and you're a front-end web developer, then that speaks of your experience level. So if you are applying for a front-end web development job, I highly recommend that you have some kind of online presence."], [0, "We've talked about having a personal brand as a developer. I highly recommend, especially for front-end developers, that you have some kind of online presence, whether that is a CodePen profile or yourownname.com. I have jonathancattrell.com, for example. Somewhere that you exist online. That could even be a Medium blog where you share a few opinions about front-end web development or share a few stories about front-end web development."], [0, "So the person is going to find you online in some way or another, whether they end up on your Facebook page or your professional website is really up to you. So take some time to craft that online presence. But aside from the point, because we're talking about resumes and we're talking about cover letters today. So if you did write a cover letter, they will probably read quickly to see if there is substance to"], [0, "to the cover letter and also to see if you've presented yourself in a relatively professional way. We'll talk more about advice for your cover letter later on in today's episode. Next, the person is likely to read over the primary bullet points in your resume and ultimately end up"], [0, "back in the browser, where they will try to find work that you've done, as well as some kind of online representation of your personality. Like I said before, they may end up on your Facebook page or your Twitter, or they could end up on your professional website. It is possible and likely that if they are doing their homework, they're going to end up on all of the above, because if they are doing their job, they're going to find out everything that they can about you online."], [0, "So some of the bullet points on your resume may end up being skipped or some of them may be focused on more heavily depending on what that company specializes in. We're going to come back after taking a quick sponsor break today and we'll discuss how to make your cover letter and your resume stand out and ultimately we're going to discuss the purpose of the resume and the cover letter. If you're just sending them"], [0, "Just because you were told to do so in some kind of college hiring program, for example, that's not a good enough reason. So we'll talk more about why you should send a resume, why you should send a cover letter, what the job of those two things are. Now, whether you're like Amanda and you're brand new to development and you're looking for an internship, or if you have years of experience, you know that it's impossible to write the flawless,"], [0, "app no matter how much testing you do no no matter how much planning you do how much design up front you do it's impossible to write a flawless application flawless apps are ultimately like flawless people they exist in theory right but i've never actually seen one in reality and if you don't have a great customer service pipeline in place"], [0, "for when your users experience those bugs, you can very easily lose a user's attention altogether. Now, most of the time people use some kind of email address to send support requests to or just a form or a link out to a website if you're in a native app."], [0, "But if you use Zendesk, Zendesk has nativ