{"timestamp": 1778579490.671525, "duration_sec": 1.721588373184204, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "But if they appear not to be, then potentially people will stop putting money into it, so the threat will just simply be gone. It's really just like supposition. But if indeed quantum computers are scalable, let's say, and accessible, then yeah, WhatsApp, I mean, messages are read, but they will be read by anyone, basically, at that point."], [0, "Will it be also messages from now that you will be able to read in the future? Or is it only in the future? Should we stop using all the internet? No, but I mean, when you think about it, like 20 years ago, we were using cryptography that is broken now. Obviously, the things that we should care about is like, is it worth storing all of the messages that are being sent around like today in order for someone to break them in like 50 years? I mean, it's not really..."], [0, "Ask the messaging companies. Yeah, I know. It is amazing. That blows my mind, that stuff, for sure. What's your talk called if people want to search and find it in the future? It's called, yeah, Taking the Quantum Leap with Go."], [0, "That's a great title. I love Quantum Leap, the TV show. Is that a reference to that? Or is it an actual Quantum Leap? It wasn't my choice. You have to ask my supervisor about that. Yeah, that's good. It's a TV show about a man that was able to go into and become other people through history. Only within his lifetime. It's not like crazy beyond the realms of possibility. And then he'd just try and make things a bit better."], [0, "Prislav, can you tell us a bit about your background and also what you're kind of recently, what you've been focused on? I tend to call myself aware of many hats because although I'm a software engineer by, you know, by sort of many years of living and breathing in the industry, I also like"], [0, "challenges of different sorts like before the pandemic when there were actual runs I would go and you know participate in long distance running challenges I also play various instruments I started playing the guitar as a teenager then I switched to a bass and been constantly looking for a band I saw you Matt are you know quite into play and you know yeah we could do a band there's just latency but maybe that's what the band could be called and everything's slightly out of time bandwidth issues yeah"], [0, "The latencies, exactly. And yeah, so the most recent challenge beyond my talk on algorithmic art was actually moving a little bit outside of the industry as sort of engineer, employee, and jumping into the, to me at least, waters of being an entrepreneur, being a boss of my own. Mm-hmm."], [0, "So, yeah, that's quite a challenge, but that actually no small part goal is in a way to blame for this because it was something that changed my path. And, you know, I'm happy to report that so far for the few months that I've been taking this path, I've been challenging the status quo and using the language in ways in which I wouldn't say it wasn't meant to solve. Actually, it was meant to solve them, but other things prevailed. Been using the language."], [0, "mainly in situations where you would see others using something like Ruby on Rails or say, I don't know, Python or something a little bit more dynamic. And I'm trying to challenge the status quo that, no, actually the language is pretty good for that. It's also pretty good for small teams as building small applications for five people, just as it's good for building Kubernetes or something that powers like a gazillion servers."], [0, "Yeah, no, absolutely. Well, your talk, you really did demonstrate that. I mean, I've never seen Go used to generate something that looked that interesting and that kind of beautiful. Obviously, this is a podcast, so, you know, we can't really rub it in too much, but I do recommend checking it out because you just won't believe some of the imagery that was created and how, you know."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["quantum computing", "cryptography", "internet security", "messaging privacy", "data encryption", "software engineering", "go programming", "algorithmic art", "generative art", "entrepreneurship", "startups", "programming languages", "web development", "open source"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
