ence and retail sectors. Download the report for analysis of vacancy rates , rent trends, construction and tenant demand."], [2, "Boston 3Q 2024 Market Report \u2013 MMG Real Estate Advisors\nWhile absorption rates have increased over the past year , new completions have slowed, leading to tighter rental market conditions. Steady leasing activity over recent quarters has sustained high occupancy levels, driving rent growth upward. With the supply side stabilizing quickly, a healthy balance of market fundamentals is emerging."], [3, "Bracing for Impact: Loan Maturities Stoke Capital Concerns\nNov 8, 2023 \u00b7 According to Trepp, the total volume of loans maturing between 2023 and 2027 is $2.8 trillion, half of that held by banks. The other half is spread across commercial mortgage-backed securities, life insurance companies, government-sponsored enterprises, debt funds, mortgage REITs and other nonbank lenders."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": null}, "prompt_chain": null, "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["boston", "central massachusetts", "commercial real estate", "real estate", "market report", "market outlook", "midyear review", "industrial property", "retail property", "office space", "multifamily housing", "life science", "vacancy rates", "rent growth", "rent trends", "construction", "new construction", "leasing activity", "absorption", "capital markets", "interest rates"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778583543.5618114, "duration_sec": 2.7099430561065674, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah, you know what it is, is like I was growing up in a very harsh environment, and I was\u2014 I didn't know I was an artist. I didn't know I was a writer, director, whatever. You know, they didn't\u2014 you didn't see those growing up where I am. And so when you're a sensitive kid, man, what you do is you build armor. Like, I was to play Mike Tyson later on, and I understood him quite well. And if you're sensitive, you, you know, anything that's precious, you put it in, you lock it in a safe, and you become the safe. And it's, it's like, I grew up, my brothers were completely different. They're engineers. So things rolled off their back. But like, for me, just\u2014 I was just volatile. And luckily, I had martial arts to kind of put my focus into. But like I said, like I was to play Mike Tyson, I understood him a great deal. And, you know, even though you take the moniker of this monster, it's only to hide what's really deep inside. And that's why you would see, if anybody's gonna go into tears in front of a million people, it's people like Mike Tyson. And you go, \"How does that fit in the same person?\" And so, that's what I was growing up. And, you know, I don't know if you know this, but I was a schoolteacher before I was an actor. Oh, really? Yeah, I taught EMDR. I was a special ed teacher. So I focused on a lot of kids who were very much like me. And I still do that. In a way, I consider that my real job. Whenever I'm off from work on a movie or whatever, I go into the inner cities. I go into community centers. I devote my time because there's nothing that I could do\u2014 there's no better spending of time than something like that because I was luckily, luckily saved. I had just at the right times in my life just different seeds planted.. And so I'm, I'm confident that if those seeds were not planted, I would not be here. Because like I say, I was, I've been through, uh, some, some crazy stuff."], [1, "It's a classic story."], [0, "Yeah, bro, man, like I tell you, like just a little under 2 years ago, a buddy of mine, who's a close friend of mine, um, he, he got out of prison. He was in prison for almost like 30 years and he found me on Facebook. And so when I went back East, we linked up. And I said\u2014 and, you know, I know a lot of people who have businesses and everything. I hooked him up, you know, got him a job. And we were sitting over lunch. And in the middle of him telling me, like, the third or fourth story, like, back in the glory days of us or whatever, while he was in the middle of this story, I was, you know, kind of getting myself set to kind of set him straight because I don't know if you want to call this superstitious, but I won't lie. I refuse to lie to my friends. I even, I won't lie by omission. So I was getting set to tell him, \"Dude, man, you got to stop embellishing on these stories. Just because you were locked up and you made these stories sound bigger than they were,\" than life. I get it. But you're\u2014 that's not real. You gotta, you gotta really, you know, kind of not do that. And in the middle of me thinking that, and I'm listening to him, I go, holy shit, he's telling the truth. I started remembering what he was telling me. And I'm like, now I'm finishing his sentences. Not only was that story true, but the other ones were true too. And dude, like, I swear, every time I think about this, I got these goosebumps. And I realize, oh my God, how close I was to being where he was. Or just not being on this planet. Right. Like, I better devote my time into helping kids the way I was helped."], [1, "Yeah, don't pull that ladder up."], [0, "No, hell no. No, hell no. Even if I'm taken out, I accept that. Even if I'm in some projects where I'm not supposed to be and I shouldn't have been, I accept that because, dude, I am abundantly lucky. Like, it's\u2014 it doesn't even\u2014 it doesn't even fit on the radar how lucky I am. And I could remember a lot of these crazy stories\u2014 you know, aside from the ones that he made me conjure back up. But man, I'm like, wow."], [1, "Well, that speaks to your character that you had downplayed it all in your mind so much that you thought he was exaggerating."], [0, "I swept it under the rug."], [1, "Yeah. I, you know\u2014 Because you're not that person anymore."], [0, "No, no. But I mean\u2014 but there was so much There were so many events, things that would\u2014 I just call it on a Wednesday that I went through that it's like, I don't know, like, I think I wouldn't trade it because I continue to be the happiest guy I know because of, I think, some of that."], [1, "Because you can appreciate the good times."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["trauma", "resilience", "personal growth", "mental health", "martial arts", "boxing", "acting", "education", "special education", "community service", "inner city", "prison reentry", "friendship", "gratitude", "identity"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778584289.5445974, "duration_sec": 1.941331386566162, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah, that's pretty much it. Yeah, that's good. I'm just like reading one or we used to. Oh, it says in here they are similar to express middleware, but they are more powerful and flexible. And I'm just wondering what is more powerful about it. It says it can register routes for you. Yeah, they're not necessarily middleware. No, not just middleware. Yeah, they can do anything. Yeah, they can add on to the Fastify interface."], [0, "I like that you can ask, like, oh, I know that you use Fastify, so let's ask you a question about Fastify. Yeah, and I did the same thing, Wes. I said, here, give me something React. Give me something difficult. And it says, in React, what's the purpose of React memo, React.memo, the higher order component, and how does it help improve the performance of a functional component? Additionally, can you provide a simple usage of this function?"], [0, "React memo will memoize sort of essentially store the output of a component or an expensive function or something that is resource intensive so that it"], [0, "when it needs to be re-rendered, it will not rerun that specific code given that the inputs for that function are exactly the same. So that could speed up the rendering, speed up any processing that happens inside of the function. How did I do for the explanation first? Perfect."], [0, "Can you provide a simple example of its usage? Yeah, you take a function that is resource intensive and you wrap it in memo or use memo hook and it will return to you a value that will not"], [0, "have to recompute itself. Yeah, that's all fine. It was asking specifically for React.memo instead of use memo, but it's the same business where you're memorizing a component rather than just a single function. Oh, I see. Yeah."], [0, "Remember React Pure Component? Yes. Yes, I do. I don't think I ever used that one. I did just because, you know, there's like blog posts about it saying it's the only way to do React. You've got to make your Pure Component. Oh, so I just looked it up. The difference between use memos, you pass it a function, but React.memo, you pass it your component and you pass it a function that will compare the props. So whether you should or should not re-render that sucker. Beauty."], [0, "Yeah, the concepts are all the same. Expert level category integration. How would you create a custom HTML element that is styled with CSS and has dynamic behavior using JavaScript? Gosh."], [0, "I don't know. Spelt? Yeah. No, you know what? I've written custom elements before, but this is the type of thing that my brain power on the syntax directly is not going to be there. I do know that typically you use attributes, you get the values of those attributes, and then you have to watch for changes in those attributes and update them yourselves. There's no like..."], [0, "did this prop change and have it automatically re-render? CSS wise, you're creating a new style sheet and you can add that CSS with JavaScript or you can, oh man, I forgot all the syntax for this. This is one of those things that you write, you don't write, and then you forget about it. So yeah, I'm going to go ahead and have a big old flop here on the actual syntax for this because my brain just isn't pulling it."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["javascript", "react", "performance optimization", "memoization", "functional components", "component rendering", "props comparison", "web components", "custom elements", "html", "css styling", "dynamic behavior", "fastify", "express middleware", "routing"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778584783.9385443, "duration_sec": 2.141655206680298, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "is that if we head off people, you know, on that, you know, this kind of violence is escalatory. And so there's a ladder of escalation before someone commits a terrorist attack like this or a hate crime. And so if we get them while they're just, you know, sort of imbibing this extremist content online and, you know, they're starting to, you know, have, you know, troubling, you know, statements or whatever. And we sort of are able to break that link before it gets to violence. That's how we're going to be able to do, you know, to prevent these crimes."], [0, "Well put, Noah Arbit, State Representative, Democrat from West Bloomfield Commerce Township, and you said something that Nolan Finley actually ended up writing about, and that is, quote, these are your words, the more anti-Jewish hate speech and anti-Zionist conspiracies are normalized and mainstream, the easier it is becoming for more people to view all Jews collectively as legitimate targets for violence."], [0, "As you and I both know, Noah, this has been happening for a while now, especially on college campuses, and they get away with it. What do we do about that? Well, I think it's incredibly concerning. And, you know, I think the biggest message that I have for your audience, and especially for people who aren't Jewish, is that, you know, there are ways to talk about, you know, no one is saying that, you know, you can't criticize the state of Israel or American policy towards Israel. No one is saying that that is anti-Semitic."], [0, "But it's the way we do it. It's the way you talk about it. There are ways that you could talk about anything where you dial up the rhetoric to 15 instead of keeping it at a five or a six, which is healthy. And the way that people are talking about Israel and the United States' relationship with Israel is incredibly conspiratorial. It's incredibly dangerous. And it fuels violence. It legitimates violence against Jewish communities. And so, like, for example, there is a profoundly different"], [0, "There's a profound difference in stating, you know, for example, take AIPAC, which is the American Israel Public Affairs Committee, a political outfit that, you know, does a lot to support candidates who are, quote, pro-Israel, whatever that means. You know, there's a difference between saying, you know, AIPAC and Zionists control the U.S. government and their influence must be quashed. That is incredibly heated and inflammatory and incendiary rhetoric versus saying, I don't agree with AIPAC."], [0, "and i think that their policies are wrong i oppose their policies and i think that they have too much influence in congress and we should hold accountable people who you know who agree with them that is an entirely different thing in terms of its impact one is conspiratorial one puts jews in danger and the other is a legitimate political critique and so what we're asking and what we need for people to do is when they're talking about these issues to be very cognizant of the language and the rhetoric that they're using"], [0, "And to make sure that when they're talking about these things, that they keep it in the space of legitimate political discourse and legitimate political critique versus conspiracies that really do a lot to further that escalation, that ladder of escalation towards eventually violence, because people get radicalized by that incendiary rhetoric."], [0, "Very well put. Noah Arbit, State Representative, Democrat from West Bloomfield Commerce Township. We look forward to our next conversation as we continue. Focus with Paul W. Smith on WJR."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["antisemitism", "hate speech", "extremism", "radicalization", "terrorism", "political violence", "online content", "conspiracy theories", "rhetoric", "israel", "zionism", "college campuses", "public policy", "political discourse", "jewish communities"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778584836.6556337, "duration_sec": 2.7461187839508057, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Tell the Redux store, I'm going to start fetching the sites. Then you'll call API.getSites, and then you'll have, like, you'll await that promise or fail it, right? And if the promise goes through and you get a list of sites, you will dispatch the, I got the sites action."], [0, "And the Redux store will update with those. And if you instead get an exception and the API failed, you'll dispatch and oops, the sites failed action and the Redux store will in a convention-based manner be updated to say that there was an error fetching this, right?"], [0, "then the UI can flow from that. The API client itself is very straightforward. It's not a lot of code. It's a small wrapper on top of the fetch API in the browser that just takes care of the error handling and pagination and"], [0, "And the core abstraction on top of fetch is maybe like 70 lines of code or something. So it's pretty lightweight in that way."], [0, "Just wanted to take a quick break to thank one of this week's sponsors, and that is DigitalOcean. So DigitalOcean is a simple developer-friendly cloud platform optimized to make managing and scaling apps easy with an intuitive API, multiple storage options, integrated firewalls, load balancers, and more."], [0, "I've personally been a customer of DigitalOcean for about five years, and I use them to host all of my server-side projects, like my custom course platform, for example, which is built with Laravel. A lot of the guests that I've had on the show in the past are DigitalOcean customers as well. For example, Taylor Otwell, the creator of Laravel, he uses DigitalOcean to host Envoyer and Laravel Forge, and Jeffrey Wei actually uses DigitalOcean to host Laracast as well."], [0, "One of DigitalOcean's newest features that I'm personally really excited about is managed databases, which lets you spin up a completely managed database server so you don't have to worry about anything like backups, managing read-only replicas, or just general server maintenance."], [0, "Now, DigitalOcean is already an extremely affordable service. You can spin up a server for as little as $5 a month, but they've been kind enough to offer a free $50 credit to Fullstack Radio listeners. So head over to do.co slash fullstack, all one word, to claim your $50 credit. And thanks to DigitalOcean for sponsoring this episode. Back to the show."], [0, "So what about the actual backend API that you are talking to from the front end? So you mentioned at the beginning of this conversation that everything kind of started as like a Rails API. Is that still the main API that powers the front end?"], [0, "So the main API is still a Rails API, a fairly traditional REST API, typically doing a lot of CRUD actions, right? Like you create a site, you list a list of sites, you get a list of deploys, you update a site with a new name and so on, right? So it's a really typical Rails API in that way. Then there are parts that are chipped into individual microservices, right?"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["redux", "state management", "javascript", "fetch api", "api client", "error handling", "asynchronous programming", "user interface", "backend api", "rails", "rest", "crud", "microservices", "cloud hosting", "managed databases"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778584851.8739817, "duration_sec": 2.74806547164917, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "You remember him spanking Tito? Yeah, he got on top of him."], [1, "Spanking him when he had him down. Randy was an animal."], [0, "Yeah, well, when we\u2014 you know, he had that heart attack while he was shooting my movie. Crazy. And then came back to set like nothing."], [1, "How did he have a fucking heart attack?"], [0, "I don't, I don't remember how exactly it was. And I think he, he drove himself to the hospital. Yeah, man, that's\u2014 talk about an American hero, man. I mean, I was there for his first fight."], [1, "Really?"], [0, "Yeah, 1997."], [1, "Yeah, I was there for his very first fight. Oh shoot, he fought this huge jack dude, took him down, mounted him, beat the shit out of him. It was wild. It was like, that was the time where wrestlers had first started cracking this code, right? There was this code of\u2014 there was a lot of people that thought like jiu-jitsu was the only way, and then the elite wrestlers got in there. Oh, the Mark Curr's, the Mark Coleman's, and then Randy, a bunch of these guys got in there. And then I was like, if a guy can just take you down and beat the fuck out of you from the top, there's not a whole lot you could do about it, right? And then we realized like, boy, that is the corner\u2014 that's the true cornerstone of martial arts. Yeah, the ability to take a guy down."], [0, "My goodness, I mean, what's harder than wrestling? I don't think there's anything."], [1, "The hardest sport in the world. Yeah. The hardest sport in the world and the best sport in the world to get your kids into at a young age because the discipline and the mental toughness that they get will carry them through for the rest of their life."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["mixed martial arts", "wrestling", "combat sports", "ufc", "grappling", "jiu jitsu", "takedowns", "ground and pound", "fighters", "sports discipline", "mental toughness", "athletic training", "sport psychology", "competition", "recovery"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778585383.2901611, "duration_sec": 2.7784616947174072, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And, you know, we don't really have that in traditional databases. So, you know, you're getting on something like Amazon EC2, you're really getting kind of commodity-like hardware. So really the way to build a big system is use lots of boxes, not one giant box. So there's definitely a fit there. And, you know, really the weak link in cloud computing right now is the data storage layer. I mean, some of these new solutions are all young. And the old solutions..."], [0, "don't map well to cloud computing. Is it fair to say that if you look at relational databases as a product class, they're all more or less doing the same thing and they do it a little bit differently, but when you talk about NoSQL, you're talking about a bigger family of systems with more variability? There's a lot more variability. I think there is some variability in the relational space, especially if you look at the reporting world where there's been a lot of custom things done with databases, but"], [0, "It's far more homogenous than this NoSQL space is. And a big reason for that is it's early with this space, right? So there's an analogy, I think, between what's happening right now in NoSQL to the early days of relational when you had Ingress and Informix and Sybase and DB2 and Oracle."], [0, "and not even everyone was using SQL as a query language. There was a bunch of relational databases that used other query languages, and then there was a bit of a shakeout, and it turned out there was a few winners, and they all used SQL. And so we're in that same kind of early shakeout phase. You know, probably some of the products in the NoSQL space don't even exist yet that will be popular in 10 years, and some of the ones there today will eventually kind of"], [0, "you know, become Informix or something, I suppose. So, but I think the space as a whole, it's growing really fast and it's really exciting."], [0, "In the relational world, there are definitely some excellent open source alternatives, but some of the very big players are pure commercial closed source, whereas in NoSQL, there is a domination of open source models and in-house databases like Amazon's DB that are provided to you as a service by a cloud provider. Do you see that persisting, or do you see large commercial entities coming to dominate the space?"], [0, "Yeah, it's very interesting. When I look at the most popular database segments, I really see what I think will be the most popular segments in, say, five years. I see three things. I see your traditional online OLTP-style relational database. That'll still be there. That'll still be totally applicable for a set of use cases. It just won't be all use cases, which it used to be."], [0, "There's also a segment which is really around data analytics, offline reporting, data warehousing, business intelligence. That's a segment, too, that exists, which is distinct and which I think will stay pretty relational. But a different set of technologies from companies like Astrodata and Greenplum and Vertica. In the third space, I think we're going to see massive use of five years from now will be NoSQL, which will really be these"], [0, "non-relational or post-relational data stores that are horizontally scalable and that are suitable mainly for online data operations."], [0, "Within the non-relational space, you've talked about classifying the systems along several different axes. One of those axes is provided by the CAP theorem. I'd like to delve a little bit into the CAP theorem so we can understand it and then use it to talk about different types of data stores. In brief, what is the CAP theorem showing?"]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["cloud computing", "data storage", "databases", "relational database", "nosql", "scalability", "distributed systems", "open source", "data analytics", "data warehousing", "business intelligence", "online transactions", "cap theorem", "consistency", "availability"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778586148.0476358, "duration_sec": 3.584779977798462, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Kritisch bewertete Winkel die aktuellen Ma\u00dfnahmen der Bundesregierung, um die B\u00fcrger von gestiegenen Energiepreisen zu entlasten, einen Tankrabatt und eine 1000-Euro-Pr\u00e4mie, die Arbeitgeber ihren Besch\u00e4ftigten steuerfrei auszahlen k\u00f6nnen. Was man grunds\u00e4tzlich sagen kann, ist, dass die Gie\u00dfkanne in Deutschland leer ist."], [0, "Also diese Zeiten, wo wir jede m\u00f6gliche exogene Krise durch hunderte Milliarden Pakete auffangen k\u00f6nnen, die ist grunds\u00e4tzlich vorbei, sagte Winkel. Wichtig sei, dass der Staat vorangehe und spare. Angestellte im \u00f6ffentlichen Dienst und Beamte sollten die Pr\u00e4mie nach Winkels Vorstellung nicht bekommen."], [0, "Ich glaube, das w\u00e4re nicht vermittelbar, auch angesichts der Gehaltserh\u00f6hung, die jetzt Beamte nochmal bekommen. Und deswegen sollte man an der Stelle mit einem guten Beispiel vorangehen. Unternehmen, die es sich leisten k\u00f6nnen, die werden diese Pr\u00e4mie auszahlen."], [0, "Das haben verschiedene Unternehmen schon gesagt, aber es werden nicht alle schaffen. Deutschland m\u00fcsse sich darauf einstellen, dass der Iran-Krieg und die Blockade der Stra\u00dfe von Hormuz anhalte, auch mit Blick auf die Kerosinversorgung. Solche Vorschl\u00e4ge wie eine Triage, die klingen nat\u00fcrlich sehr, sehr radikal."], [0, "Aber nat\u00fcrlich muss man sich \u00fcberlegen, wie k\u00f6nnen wir mit einem knappen Gut, was um 20 Prozent verknappt wird, sparsamer umgehen, sagte Winkel. Es bringe nichts, in Panik auszubrechen, aber die angespannte Lage m\u00fcsse ernst genommen werden. Bundeswirtschaftsministerin Katharina Reichel, CDU, bittet am Montag Branchen- und Beh\u00f6rdenvertreter zu einer Krisensitzung, Bundeskanzler Friedrich Merz, CDU, will zeitnah den Nationalen Sicherheitsrat einberufen."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["energy prices", "government policy", "economic relief", "fuel subsidy", "inflation", "public spending", "fiscal austerity", "tax free bonus", "public sector wages", "energy crisis", "oil supply", "geopolitics", "iran conflict", "supply shortages", "crisis management"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
