Stephen Box and all Vanguard Tactics saying, leave the gun, take the cannoli."], [1, "Have a good week. See you later."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Gaming"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["podcasting", "youtube", "content creation", "audience growth", "subscriber goals", "work schedule", "episode planning", "tabletop games", "miniature wargaming", "warhammer", "rules discussion", "codex", "new releases", "community support"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778602735.4051535, "duration_sec": 2.111015558242798, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "I think a lot of JavaScript developers already know, you know, kind of a lot of the building blocks of functional programming, right? I mean, things like, you know, John, you mentioned Ramda, which must admit I'm not that familiar with, but, you know, I've used underscore and I think a lot of people use underscore, right? And, you know, underscore, you know, emphasizes things like maps and folds and reducers and these kinds of things. And they really are the building blocks, right? Like putting functions together, higher order functions and assembling programs this way."], [0, "And to a certain extent, you know, after that, everything else is, you know, there's various techniques and whatnot, but everything else is sort of building up abstractions and things on top of that. So absolutely, I think, you know, people should try and come to PureScript that way. As you build these abstractions, you know, a good type system really helps a lot, I think, because you can, you start assembling these pieces. And as John mentioned, you want this sort of local reasoning property where"], [0, "it's easy to reason about one map or one folder or a handful, right? But then as you sort of start assembling this into a large program, you don't want to have to keep all that structure in your head, right? So that's where I think something like PureScript comes in handy. Can I offer another perspective on that question, Amy? Yeah, sure. So, well, I guess it's different from the question you actually asked, which is like, if I know JavaScript, what do I do to prepare myself to get into PureScript? But I think in some ways it can be easier to learn functional programming if you are still learning programming"], [0, "I know for me, I came to functional programming after I felt pretty comfortable doing, I guess, imperative programming. And I was used to feeling like I knew what was going on and I understood everything. And I'd kind of forgotten the feeling of hearing a concept and not really understanding it well and not seeing what it was used for. And so it took me a little bit of effort to get used to being confused again, I guess. So I think"], [0, "if you're learning programming for the first time just programming in general you're used to working to understand things and I had gotten lazy so I think it could almost be easier to learn functional programming if you're starting off at the beginning of your journey or career or whatever because you're used to having to work to understand stuff and there are all these new concepts that are new no matter what or they're new if you've been programming for a while and doing imperative programming or they're new if you haven't been programming at all they're like the same amount of newness so it's helpful to just like"], [0, "chill out and say, it's okay if I don't understand stuff. I'm going to work to understand it. I think that's the most important thing. It's just having that attitude of saying, yeah, I don't understand this, but I'm not going to be afraid. And I can speak, again, as someone who's been programming for a long time, the well is so enormously deep that I'm never going to know even a tiny fraction of all there is to know about computer programming. And in order to sort of have skills that stay current and"], [0, "You just need to sort of give up the idea that you're ever going to be a master of programming. It's always going to be this continuous process of learning. And the only way that can happen is if we sort of let go of some of that fear of not knowing and end up being confused and really dedicate ourselves to working through it. It's okay to be confused. It's okay to not know what this means. Sometimes we just need to roll up our sleeves and dig in and have faith and confidence that we'll come out the other side. And in fact, we"], [0, "We do, in most cases, come out the other side, having leveled up as a software engineer. Yeah, I think it's pretty interesting that recently there's been even a greater focus on functional programming and JavaScript. Yeah. And so seeing that hopefully makes people more interested in concepts like this. But there's also plenty of talk about the fact that, hey, if you go and learn a new paradigm like functional, it'll level up your programming. And it's a very widespread approach."], [0, "thought and it is really cool even when you have been doing something for a long time to go in and do something new we like that generally which is a reason why a lot of people get into programming and it's great i think it's actually enjoyable to be in a situation where i feel very lost and uh trying to learn something new and i've had 20 years of it it never stops yeah"], [0, "So part of me asking that question, too, because I was curious about how it related to Elm. When we did the Elm episode and I looked around at Elm a little bit, and I've still looked around even after we had the episode, and it's been very approachable for someone who's newer. So that was part of why I had asked that question and was curious how you felt PureScript related to Elm. So I think originally the way I thought about this a couple of years ago when PureScript was first starting,"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["functional programming", "javascript", "purescript", "elm", "type system", "higher order functions", "abstraction", "code composition", "local reasoning", "programming paradigms", "learning programming", "software engineering", "programming education", "developer mindset", "libraries"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778603138.3773134, "duration_sec": 3.445300817489624, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "If you want to take a look at any of the information, if I get a chance to record it, I will. Our next user group meeting is September 17th. So I don't know if this episode will come out before or after, but I'll try to get a recording of the Elm presentation. It's not going to be great because I'm not great at Elm yet, but maybe somebody can at least learn enough. If you want to learn Elm, I really recommend the Pragmatic Studios Elm course. I found that to be a blast."], [0, "And if you're anywhere in the Erie, Pennsylvania area on the 17th, our meetup is going to be taking place at the Radius coworking space. So feel free to stop by and, you know, we love new members. So hopefully we'll have some food and we can hang out and have a good time. Awesome. Well, yeah, it's been awesome having you on the show as always. It's fun to catch up with you. I know we don't really chat too often outside of these podcast episodes. So it's always fun to chat with you and talk about some of the stuff that we've been working on over the last couple months in between."], [0, "Yeah, definitely. Maybe we can get a Slack channel again now that IRC's kind of faded out. Right on, man. Well, if anyone wants to check out show notes for this episode where there'll probably be some cool links to some cool functional programming stuff and things like that, then I think it'll be at fullstackradio.com slash 25. I switched the hosting for Fullstack Radio now, so the URLs have changed slightly, but the old ones still work thanks to Simplecast, who were kind enough to set up the redirects for me when I switched the hosting over to them, so..."], [0, "Props to those guys. If you run a podcast and you're hosting it on Libsyn or something, you should switch to Simplecast. They're the best. So if you want to check out the show notes, they'll be at fullstackradio.com slash 25. Thanks to Laracast for sponsoring the podcast. If you want to learn more about Laravel or PHP or any web application development stuff in general, check out laracast.com. I've been a subscriber since day one, and I find cool new stuff to learn there pretty much every week. So there's something there for everybody, so definitely check it out. Thanks, everyone. See you next time."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["functional programming", "elm", "web development", "podcasting", "meetup", "user group", "programming course", "software engineering", "laravel", "php", "coworking space", "online learning", "developer community", "show notes", "hosting platform"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778603246.8207302, "duration_sec": 2.5234460830688477, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "So I filter out any of the violations that are on lines that didn't change. Then I map those violations into these comment objects. And then those comment objects get posted to the GitHub API. And that's like the conceptual flow of the whole thing. And you don't have to have an if statement anywhere, even though there's conditional logic, really. You know what I mean? So it's pretty cool."], [0, "That sounds like a fun project. Yeah, it was pretty fun, actually. There's some interesting things that I learned that I didn't expect to learn, like understanding the diff file format syntax and what the numbers at the top of a diff mean and how to parse that and figure out what lines the changes are actually on in the real file and stuff. So there's some cool little things in there like that, for sure."], [0, "You know who Michael Feathers is? Yes. The guy who wrote Working Effectively with Legacy Code? I haven't actually read that book, but I've heard lots of good things about it. So the other day I decided to kind of deep dive into his stuff and just read what he's all about, right? And I stumbled across his blog and he's been talking a lot about this compositional chain stuff that we're talking about and how he's trying to figure out, taking it to its logical extremes and trying to solve every possible problem he can using these compositional chains. Yeah."], [0, "and um he had this really cool blog post that i'll put in the show notes about generating guitar tab so you do you play guitar or bass or anything yeah guitar yeah yeah so you know how to read guitar tab i'm assuming like every guitar player in the world does right so he's talking about how like you know guitar tab is actually a pain in the ass to actually write out by hand because you have to like you know dash dash dash dash put in the number and like"], [0, "It's actually quite annoying to type out. And he was thinking, well, wouldn't it be cool if I could type guitar tab by instead just having like a big long file where each line just contained a string number, a space, and then the fret number that I wanted to play on that string. And it was just a big long file full of these things. And somehow I want to turn that into guitar tab at the end of the day."], [0, "so he wrote like this like six line ruby script that is all using these compositional chains to take that file and transform it into guitar tab and it was it was really really cool the solution that he came up with using things like transpose right like where you can kind of like invert the way that the array works and it was it was really clever so i'll definitely have to link to that it's really cool"], [0, "Ruby has a nice library for array and innumerable manipulations. Like it's a lot richer than what's in PHP right now. It's like super good. And it's nothing that PHP can't have. It's just like the standard library doesn't have them right now. So like Taylor's augmented it a little bit in his stuff, like for array gets and different things like that."], [0, "Yeah, I think that's like the thing that I wish we had the most in PHP is like these native array objects where I could call methods on an array because I can't imagine ever working on a PHP project where I didn't have some sort of collection object to encapsulate like every array that I ever work with ever because I never want to have to have these like inside out array function calls where if I have to filter something, then reduce it and then map it."], [0, "I want to write it in that order. I don't want it to be like filter on the inside, then, you know, map at the next level out and then reduce on the next level out from that. It just reads so poorly. So I can't imagine like not having that. And it's when you see some of the solutions, especially in Ruby, where you can use like symbols to represent like method calls and stuff. You have these like one liners that are like less than 80 characters long that might do five transformations. And it looks totally awesome. Yeah."], [0, "Right. Symbol to proc is like my favorite thing that exists. And it's actually faster at the compiler level. So if you can use it, you use it. Is that when you put like the ampersand in front of the symbol? Yeah. Yeah. It just tells the Ruby interpreter to process that as a proc. So when the symbol gets passed through, it'll transform it, makes a little bytecode level block out of it, and then runs it. That's awesome. So it's pretty neat."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["functional programming", "compositional chains", "code quality", "static analysis", "git diff", "github api", "ruby", "php", "arrays", "collections", "data transformation", "scripting", "guitar", "music notation", "legacy code"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
