{"timestamp": 1778607235.6829844, "duration_sec": 2.109701633453369, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "I know when I smoke menthol, I sound like Katt Williams the next day. They ruined\u2014 that's one of the reasons I had to downgrade to like\u2014 some people think that I started smoking Marlboro Lights because I started dating white women, which is more appealing. Unless you date a white woman from the Midwest, then she probably smoking Newports and drinking Pepsis and Coca-Cola just like you. But I think that's probably\u2014 but I got so many bad habits that I need to change."], [1, "Here we go. And our sponsor, our AI sponsor Perplexity, says the menthol effect in cigarettes come from the chemical menthol itself, which is added as a flavoring and a sensory agent to the tobacco. Menthol is naturally found in peppermint and other mint plants, can also be made synthetically in a lab. Menthol activates cold-sensitive nerve receptors in the mouth, throat, and airways, creating a cooling sensation when you inhale smoke.. It's a mild anesthetic numbing effect that reduces pain and irritation from hot, harsh cigarette smoke, making it feel smoother. Menthol can suppress the cough reflex and dull early warnings or early warning signs of airway irritation, which make it easier to inhale more deeply and more often. Menthol reduces the perceived harshness of nicotine and smoke. The minty taste and smell plus the cooling feel act as pleasant sensory cues that many smokers come to associate with satisfaction and craving. Menthol can alter nicotine metabolism and the way nicotine acts on brain receptors, which may increase nicotine's reinforcing addictive effects. In short, the menthol effect is not from nicotine but from added menthol, which cools and numbs the airways, masks irritation, and can make cigarettes feel smoother and more addictive without making them any safer."], [0, "So menthol cigarettes appeal to Black people because it's a cool cigarette. It's cool. That's what\u2014 so much sense why the brand Cool cigarette, because it makes it\u2014 that makes sense."], [1, "That's why they called it Cool, I bet."], [0, "Damn, what white people do to destroy my community, man."], [1, "Destroy everybody. Yeah, they don't give a fuck about anybody. Half ain't wide body, ain't Soul, mild ain't bold, cool ain't cool, Newport is. Oh, because that was like a\u2014 that makes sense. Cools, because people used to smoke Cools. Do Cools exist anymore in jail?"], [0, "Only in jail? I think that, I think, I don't know what the ratio is, what cigarette gets you more money in a dice game. But whenever I hear people telling war stories, they like, man, I got a pack, I got a carton of Kools for a bag of Doritos or something. But the value of a Kool cigarette is higher in prison."], [1, "Isn't it crazy that they give you cigarettes in prison? That's crazy. It's like the only drug you can get in prison."], [0, "Yeah."], [1, "And those, you can't get alcohol, right?"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["menthol", "cigarettes", "smoking", "nicotine", "addiction", "health", "throat", "sensory effects", "marketing", "race", "culture", "prison", "jail", "substance use", "tobacco brands"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778614028.7437127, "duration_sec": 3.7956812381744385, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Joining us now to talk about what everybody's talking about and a lot of us are sneezing about, and that's the pollen situation, Sean Taylor, who is with the North Carolina Department of Environmental Quality, joins us now. Sean, thanks for being with us."], [0, "Thank you for having me. Well, before we talk about how this year's pollen season is in a more formal way, most of us are measuring it by the fact that most of us now have yellow cars that we didn't plan for and that sort of thing. But give me a quick overview of how the Department of Environmental Quality actually measures pollen. Yeah, at DEQ's Division of Air Quality, we turn the pollen in the air into trusted numbers that North Carolinians can use. So at our lab at"], [0, "Reedy Creek in Raleigh, we have a pollen sampler that takes a 24-hour sample of the pollen, spins around really fast, puts the grains directly onto a slide that they then look at under a microscope, and they count the individual grains of pollen. It could be a time-consuming process if there's a lot of pollen in the air like there is in recent days."], [0, "And they identify the pollen using different surface features of those grains. And my understanding is that you're actually getting an upgrade in how you do that because there's some renovations going on, right? There are renovations going on at the lab. They're kind of reconstructing a lot of the buildings there. And actually that's at times interfering with our equipment right now because our sampler is whirling in the background of all of this construction activity."], [0, "Now, help me to understand, what do we know right now? Like I said, the pollen situation, you know, we can see it, we can feel it, we can sneeze it. But what, if anything, do we know so far about what this spring pollen season may look like? So we are already seeing very high levels of tree pollen in Raleigh. You know, there's a lot of trees in North Carolina. Raleigh is the city of Oaks."], [0, "We typically see that tree pollen peak at our very high levels in late March or early April. We're seeing that right now. Today was the highest pollen count that we've had so far in 2026. Yesterday was the second highest. So that's typical. That's what we've seen in years past. That's eventually going to taper off based on what we see in past years."], [0, "And we'll eventually see coming into May and June an increase in grass pollen as that tree pollen kind of takes the backseat. So North Carolina, we really talk about pollen season more as a relay race between different plant species. And we'll see all the way through late fall into September and October when it's ragweed that we'll be talking about different pollen and different species producing different levels of pollen."], [0, "And depending on how that might impact you and your allergies, you just need to pay attention to those numbers. And that's why we count them and report them every day. At the risk of getting too technical, how are they measured? How do we know if it's a lot of pollen or a little bit of pollen? You mentioned the fact that they run through these spindles, so to speak, and they're put under microscopes. But is there some numerical way to measure this?"], [0, "It's literally somebody sitting at a microscope counting individual grains of pollen. They look at the slide and they tally them up. When we get to very high counts, like we had today, over 1,500 grains in our sample, they do use estimation techniques to extrapolate how many grains are in that sample. But for most of the year, they're sitting there counting the grains and then they do"], [0, "also note what species were the most prevalent in that sample. And the weather right now, we're in a bit of a drought situation. Does weather impact how much pollen is generated and how much impact it has? It can, but it's complicated. I mean, there's different species. The geography makes a difference. So it's really difficult to really say what impact"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["pollen season", "pollen counts", "air quality", "north carolina", "raleigh", "department of environmental quality", "laboratory testing", "pollen sampler", "microscope analysis", "tree pollen", "grass pollen", "ragweed", "allergy symptoms", "environmental monitoring", "construction renovations", "drought", "botany", "public health"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
