mond L. Flynn Marine Park Master Plan Update\nThe TAC will review proposed development projects, provide feedback on each project's transportation analysis and impacts and monitor the implementation of the ..."], [3, "Planning Department Advances Zoning Reforms In ...\nFlynn Marine Park enabled a new commuter water shuttle stop in partnership with the Massachusetts Convention Center Authority and the Seaport ..."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": null}, "prompt_chain": null, "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["boston", "seaport", "marine park", "marine industrial park", "commercial real estate", "office development", "lab space", "property development", "industrial real estate", "manufacturing facility", "industrial zoning", "zoning reform", "city planning", "planning department", "land use", "urban development", "waterfront infrastructure", "coastal infrastructure", "climate resilience", "water shuttle/ferry service"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778615907.1135306, "duration_sec": 2.0795161724090576, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Because the culture is not only within the people, but you can feel it within the product also because it will resonate with people. So, you know, it's embedded in the entire dagger history. Yeah. Actually, I feel like the interactions with the community, you know, there's the team and then there's the community and they're very connected. You know, one really feels like an extension of the other."], [0, "And it just kind of happened that way because of how we work. But these days, what's on my mind a lot is just strengthening that. And you made me think of that, Eric, when you said it's embedded in the product. It feels like it's already been done to have a product that's open source and has a great community. And you use the product and you talk about it with the community and you kind of go back and forth. And"], [0, "That's great. We were part of that with Docker. Others did that too. Everyone's pushing the envelope. We want to push the envelope even further. We mentioned we're doing even more in the open. When you join our Discord server, it doesn't feel like you're joining a meetup or a community room. It feels like you're literally joining our workplace. That's where we work. Sure, yeah, there's a private channel where we exchange memes or whatever, but..."], [0, "When we talk about developing the product as a team, we do it in the open and people are there, they listen, they participate. I feel like the next step is somehow building that into the product experience itself so that there shouldn't be a dividing line between I'm interacting with the community and, oh, I'm using the product. I think it could be, the community itself should be part of the product in some way. I know this is kind of vague what I'm saying, but"], [0, "No, it does make sense. And especially when it comes to paying users and free users, there should be as little difference as possible, hopefully none, other than you just pay for Dagger because you believe in it and you want it to succeed and you want to be part of it a more inclusive way. I mean, you just literally want to be part of Dagger success. And that's one way that you can do that. I mean, if you derive genuine value and it saves you money. But you mean like donations?"], [0, "No, I mean like paying for it. It's called a subscription. Here's a double idea. Let me use my card to pay for it. Just because you're paying shouldn't mean you're leaving the community and now you're in another group of people more serious because there's money involved, you know? Exactly. It's like team and community. Yeah. When like, for example,"], [0, "Helder. Helder is a member of the team who we met through the community. He joined and he started using it, giving us feedback, really good feedback, and then feature suggestions and then pull requests."], [0, "And then, you know, when they joined the team, that was great. He didn't leave the community when he joined the team. He just like went even closer to the core of the community. So it has to be the same with customers. And it did not feel that way at Docker, for sure. There was a big, big division, at least in the way we looked at it at Docker. If you were talking, you were either talking to someone in the community and then that went through the community people, or you talked to the customers and that went through the customers people, you know, and..."], [0, "It was almost like two different companies. And that was a big mistake because it turns out a lot of the customers, they're actually under free time geeking out on the community site. There's a lot of cross-pollination. And if you ignore that, you're missing out the big picture. So this to me sounds like a great takeaway. And I'm wondering if there's a few others that we can share as we prepare to wrap up. What are our takeaways for Tiger users, present or future?"], [0, "and even maybe to our future selves. I really like that idea. We are looking to expand the team even more. We are hiring. You will have fun. You will have fun for sure. Building some doors."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["open source", "community building", "product culture", "developer community", "discord", "product development", "user feedback", "subscriptions", "free users", "paying customers", "team growth", "hiring", "docker", "customer success", "business model", "collaboration", "user experience", "organizational culture"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778616470.5104537, "duration_sec": 3.602592945098877, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "She gets five consecutive life sentences in Idaho, no parole ever, plus 10 years for grand theft because she kept collecting her dead children's Social Security benefits while they were buried in her husband's backyard. Her appellate attorney, Craig Durham, filed a brief in May of 25 laying out five constitutional violations. He says entitle her to a new trial or outright dismissal."], [0, "In January 26, the state had fired back. Deputy Attorney General Jeff Nye files a 59-page response that systematically dismantles every one of those claims. And now, as of early April 26, Lori's reply brief has been filed, and the Idaho Supreme Court has the case. No date for oral arguments, no timeline for a decision. It could take months. It could take longer. But..."], [0, "Well, all of that appellate machinery was grinding along in Idaho. Lori was busy in Arizona."], [0, "And what happened there should tell the justices and all of us exactly what kind of defendant they're dealing with. Let's go back to April of last year, 25. She went to trial in Maricopa County for conspiring to kill Charles Vallow, her fourth husband. Remember this one? Charles was shot and killed by Lori's brother, Alex Cox, in her Chandler home in July of 2019. She claimed self-defense on Alex's behalf."], [0, "Prosecutors said she orchestrated it for insurance money and to clear the path for Chad Daybell. She represented herself there. She's not a lawyer. The jury deliberated for about three hours and came back guilty. Then in June of 25, she went to trial again. Same courthouse, same judge for conspiring to kill Brandon Boudreaux, her niece's ex-husband. Someone fired a rifle at Boudreaux from the back of Lori's Jeep while he was sitting in his car outside his home."], [0, "Missed his head by a few inches. Again, she represented herself because she's just that bomb diggity lawyer. Or she's not. Again, she called no witnesses. And again, she presented no evidence. The jury deliberated 30 minutes. 30 minutes. That's less than the time most people spend deciding what to have for dinner. And then in July of 25, Judge Justin Bursky..."], [0, "sentenced her to two consecutive life terms with the possibility of parole after 25 years on each, stacked on top of the Idaho sentences. And before he did, he looked at her and said something that should echo through every filing in her Idaho appeal."], [0, "told her she had shown blatant disregard for humanity. He told her the amount of contemplation, calculation, planning, and manipulation that went into her crimes was unparalleled in his career. He told her she should never be released from prison. And then he said this, that eventually the cameras she seeks out and the media attention she craves would fade and she would drift into obscurity."], [0, "That's the woman asking Idaho's highest court for another chance. And her behavior in those Arizona courtrooms wasn't just legally unsuccessful. It was also a masterclass in why the Idaho trial court's decisions were correct."], [0, "In the Charles Vallow trial, jurors told reporters afterward that Lori spent most of the proceedings smiling and laughing. One juror said she didn't seem to take anything seriously. Another juror said he was initially on the fence but swung to guilty after the prosecution's closing."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["life sentence", "idaho court", "parole", "grand theft", "social security benefits", "appellate brief", "constitutional violations", "idaho supreme court", "arizona trial", "self representation", "conspiracy to murder", "jury deliberation", "criminal justice", "insurance fraud", "media attention", "legal strategy", "courtroom behavior", "sentencing remarks"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778616983.8526976, "duration_sec": 1.983241319656372, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "So trust is not the only factor for relationships. But I do want to break down trust a little bit and talk about what it means to be trustworthy. Well, first, to be trustworthy, you have to be dependable. You have to be dependable. You have to follow through on your commitments."], [0, "be where you said you would be when you said you would be there if you can't make it in time tell the people that are waiting on you and this this is true for any commitment that you make if you make a commitment follow through on it whether that's a meeting or if you say you're going to finish a task at a certain point in time try your best to follow through with it don't blatantly lie to the people around you that is not dependable"], [0, "And it's very easy to do this wrong accidentally. It's very easy to be undependable accidentally. You do that by having good intentions, by having good intentions and overextending yourself. If you overextend yourself,"], [0, "You're having good intentions. You're saying that you want to be a good person, that you want to be a trustworthy person, but you overextend yourself and you say you're going to be able to do one thing and you're unable to fulfill that commitment. So you are undependable even though your intentions were good. Okay? You're undependable even though your intentions were good. And now..."], [0, "Even though I want to be able to trust you, even though your intentions were good, I can't. You're not trustworthy because you overextend yourself. And I don't know how much of what you say you're going to be able to do, you're actually going to be able to do. So that first factor of trust is dependability. The second factor is honesty and communication."], [0, "honesty and communication. If you can't finish something on time, it's far better to be honest and upfront than to wait until the due date to tell that you're not done with that particular thing, right? It is much better to be honest and upfront. Honesty is incredibly important. Consider your word, your bond."], [0, "Don't make promises you can't keep. This is obviously related to dependability. Be careful that you don't read this as don't make promises at all. Don't read it as that. You need to be making promises so that people know they can depend on you. It's important that you do make solid, dependable commitments and follow through on them. Making no commitments may technically make you honest,"], [0, "but it's not going to get you very far if people can't depend on you to produce something. If you haven't built a track record, it's like building credit with your friends and your colleagues. If you never actually make commitments, then you can technically say that you never break commitments."], [0, "but that doesn't make you any more trustworthy. I need to see a dependable, solid, built-up track record of you doing what you said you would do. So I'd much rather you have a very conservative estimate that you're willing to commit to than an overextended estimate or no estimate at all."], [0, "Of course, we lumped in communication here with trust. We could go on and on about communication. There are entire books written about the nuances of communication. And so we can't really cover all that is necessary to cover in one episode or even in five episodes about communication because it is such a complex topic."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["trust", "relationships", "trustworthiness", "dependability", "commitments", "follow through", "reliability", "overextending", "honesty", "communication", "promises", "track record", "time management", "accountability", "workplace", "credit building", "decision making", "personal integrity"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778617710.5387387, "duration_sec": 3.2507293224334717, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And then finally, the last thing people need to be trained on is we do, and this goes back to taking the car keys away, IT has always been, I used to call us the wall, wall, bus, sheriff. We're sitting there watching the tumbleweeds go by, and we really feel like we've got the badge and the hat and the six-shooter, and we're going to secure everything."], [0, "And that's not today's world. It's not that easy. We don't have the keys to the kingdom anymore. So if you're constantly going to jump out and tell people, oh, you can't do this, you can't do that, oh, you're not allowed to do this,"], [0, "People are going to go do that anyway. They're going to go spin up an AWS instance. They're going to put data somewhere it shouldn't be. And they're going to say, oh, well, I put all my stuff on Dropbox anyway. So, oops, I guess I don't care anymore. And now you're in the newspaper because all of your organization's data is on Box, right? Yeah."], [0, "So it's really about turning that security conversation from, you've been bad, I'm going to punish you, or I'm going to make fun of you because you didn't do the right thing, and making it constructive. What is the organization trying to do? How can we make that secure? That's the conversation we need to have. And the same thing goes with users. We spend on, oh, don't click on this, don't click on that. Oh, you can't go to this webpage, right?"], [0, "What are you trying to do? Why do you want to go here? And how do we protect you from yourselves? How do we know that you're going into the Internet's a malicious, hostile environment? We know you're going into it. How do we best protect you and not just blame you for doing something anybody else would do? You know, one of the examples I use is..."], [0, "We do some penetration testing and the number one most effective, absolutely free penetration test that I have found and is 100% effective is you get malware, you put in an Excel file, you name the Excel file, senior executive salaries, and you email it to everybody in the company and you wait about five minutes and somebody opens that and you're inside of the company."], [0, "And that's not something magic away. Somebody somewhere is going to see an Excel file that says, you know, executive compensation, employee salaries. Somebody is going to click on that. It is human nature. You can't legislate that away. You can't train that away. So figuring out how to protect people from themselves is critical. So I know that was kind of long-winded for what's a quick high-level answer, but those are really the first three places I would start with. What can I do about insider threat?"], [0, "So the example you just gave strikes me as rather intractable. What are practical things that you can do to keep people from clicking on a link that is so irresistibly curiosity-inducing as that?"], [0, "I think that's exactly how I would reframe that because you can't get people to stop clicking on that link. It's an impossible task. Unless you're going to do some kind of clockwork orange, tape people's eyes open and make them watch violent movies situation."], [0, "You've got to change the game. And the game is, how do I protect that application? How do I protect that environment, that computer? And how do I make sure that if somebody does accidentally click on that link and get compromised, that who's ever compromised that laptop?"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["cybersecurity", "security training", "insider threat", "cloud computing", "shadow it", "data leakage", "access control", "user behavior", "phishing attacks", "malware", "penetration testing", "endpoint protection", "risk management", "organizational culture", "compliance", "employee communication", "aws", "dropbox"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778617999.9265459, "duration_sec": 3.348773717880249, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Every day, thousands of people, mostly American, mostly white, and mostly retired, come to this town looking for relief from the turmoil in their mouths. Here, dental care costs up to 80% less than what it might cost in the US. Dental tourism to Los Algodones is so common that many call this place Molar City. Today, there are nearly 1,000 dentists in this 7,000-person town, and 98% of those in the patient's chair traveled here from outside Mexico. Lasha is going to take it from here."], [1, "Watch any one of these YouTube videos, and you'll see how a trip to Molar City is made easy for an American. For decades, this crossing didn't even require a passport. In one video, a retired couple arrives at Arizona's edge. They do a 360, displaying the massive lot where they've just parked. \"We love Mexico,\" they say emphatically, talking to the camera. The camera then pans to show the rust-colored border wall in front of them. That corrugated monstrosity. They marvel. Then a US Border Patrol vehicle starts driving past the screen, on guard, it seems, for anyone trying to cross in the opposite direction. To this, the camera woman speaks again, as if she's talking directly to Border Patrol. \"Get 'em, boys,\" she says. Then they begin their walk into Mexico. In Los Algodones, there are dense clusters of medical offices and a canopy of billboards above them. Vendors are set up on sidewalks where they sell handmade goods and kitschy souvenirs, like this one graphic tee that says, \"Keep calm, you're on the fun side of Trump's wall.\" And then there are the street promoters, people who are stationed at almost every street corner. Each of them competing for superlatives: the best deal, the quickest appointment, the most popular clinic in town. In Molar City, a street promoter is the first person a tourist will encounter."], [2, "Good morning, buddy."], [3, "What is it? Dental?"], [1, "Pharmacy?"], [3, "Glasses?"], [2, "I got dental promotion."], [1, "Some promoters try to lure people in with discounts. Others vie for attention in more creative ways. Like dressing up in a full-body tooth costume and waving a sign. Then there's Alberto, a street promoter who takes a different approach altogether. He tries to attract customers with humor."], [3, "I'm always trying to make stuff funny, you know, make them laugh like a comedian almost, make them relax just for that second."], [1, "Alberto is employed by a couple clinics in town. He's assigned to a specific street corner where he stands all day and tries to make his commissions."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["dental tourism", "los algodones", "molar city", "dental care", "low cost treatment", "american retirees", "border crossing", "mexico united states border", "dental clinics", "street promoters", "medical offices", "youtube videos", "healthcare marketing", "immigration policy", "small town economy", "cultural stereotypes", "travel planning", "humor advertising"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778618126.6044507, "duration_sec": 2.7360458374023438, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[4, "I think it's completely normal for someone leaving their home country and accessing healthcare to be questioning, you know, is this is this safe? What are the standards of care in this different country? And then I think there's another layer on top of that where I think they're also trying to combat really prejudiced assumptions about Mexico as much as possible, that it's, you know, a country from the Global South, that it's underdeveloped or even undeveloped, that there's no way the standards could be as high of quality in a place like Mexico."], [1, "Cristina says that clinics in Algodones employ certain strategies to maintain a positive reputation with tourists. Some clinics will pay medical tourism companies in other countries to act as a middleman between patient and clinic. Many of these companies are based in Europe, and they'll put Mexican clinics through a vetting process to prove their legitimacy to a non-Mexican clientele. When making interior design choices, clinics might consider an American aesthetic, whatever that means. The goal is to make sure the image of Mexican dental care is squeaky clean. Sometimes quite literally. Cristina remembers being hit by the intense smell of sanitizer when she walked into the many clinics in town."], [4, "[Speaker:Cristina] It was overwhelming sometimes walking into a dental clinic, just the smell of sanitization. Like, it feels like above and beyond anything I'd ever experienced myself walking into healthcare spaces. Like, they're really trying to make sure you smell and notice right away, like, this is clean."], [1, "[Speaker:Lulu] All this effort is to make Los Algodones feel safe. And comfortable, relatable even. And so there are ways in which the town has worked to play up its Americanness by minimizing its Mexicanness."], [4, "I definitely remember this sense of, like, staff being told to minimize their Mexican accents because that would help people, tourists, feel more comfortable."], [1, "Christina's research also found that some clinics would hire someone who speaks American English to just sit in the waiting room. The idea being that their presence might help nervous Americans feel more at ease. It seems like everyone who lives and works in town has their own strategy to calm anxious American nerves. One local dental tourism company uses a camera to occasionally livestream the border crossing from a bird's-eye view. These videos exist almost as if to say, \"See? It's so\u2014 uneventful here. Boring, in fact.\" safe. Back when Dr. Jesus Medina was head of the tourism department in the '80s, he developed his own strategy to make skeptical US tourists feel comfortable in a Mexican dental clinic. He created a complaint office, a place tourists could go if they felt like they were being scammed. He called it the Office of Defense of the tourists."], [2, "That way, if one Mexican doctor do a bad job to one of the patients, they can go to that office, you know? And then we call the doctor and we make them give the money back to the patients, you know?"], [1, "The office functioned like an amateur courtroom. And Medina enlisted Dr. Maga\u00f1a as the judge. Maga\u00f1a would inspect the dental work of other Mexican dentists \u2014occasionally calling the police in for backup."], [2, "It was very hard, you know. We seen the police and comes there. He says\u2014and Mariana says\u2014your work is not good. You have to give the money back to the lady."], [1, "Police in Los Algodones exist to protect the industry and to make tourists feel safe. There's an old promotional video for Molar City still up on YouTube. It's from 12 years ago, and it's less than a minute long. In the clip, a police officer does a salsa twirl with an elderly white woman. The video is titled \"All Tourists Are Safe and Fun in Los Lagodones.\"."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["medical tourism", "dental clinics", "mexico healthcare", "los algodones", "patient safety", "quality standards", "reputation management", "clinic sanitation", "cultural stereotypes", "american tourists", "border crossing", "complaint office", "interior design", "healthcare marketing", "tourism industry", "consumer protection", "language accents", "police presence"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
