t changes our lives in a very big way to focus, to accentuate the positive. We heard that somewhere before, didn't we? We've got great thanks for you choosing to be a part of the best and brightest audience in all of radio, which happens when you listen to WJR, whether you're getting your daily dose of Dietz, I'll talk with Kevin Dietz."], [0, "Before us or right after us, J.R. Afternoon with Ryan Armani into Mitch Albom and sports rap and all the other great programming. 24 hours a day starting your morning off with J.R. Mornings. That's for sure. That's a great way to get going. Meanwhile, we've got a lot going on. We've got some nice guests coming up."], [0, "We are streaming live Facebook, YouTube pages, live video streaming. I do hope that you'll get the free WJR app and you can follow us along. You can stream us. You can also do the podcast if you can't be here noon to two like we are Monday through Friday. All right, let me look at some of the ideas that Mike has passed along. Well, we know at least three ships have come under attack. It's probably more than that now."], [0, "Very nerve-wracking to think we're going to escort ships through the Strait of Hormuz because they're sitting ducks there, rather literally. What happened with the Consumer Price Index? Did they come out with anything? I know they were about to or supposed to. You know, that's a good question. I thought that would have been bigger news. Well, it must have been good news if the news media is not covering it. We'll see. Let's see. You know..."], [0, "There was a story here that indicated to me, well, I can't find it. February inflation was unchanged, so it sounds like it was just no good news, no great news, just news. And no bad news. Yeah."], [0, "Germany, Japan, to unblock oil reserves as G7 stands ready to act. There are like 23 or 32 countries who are throwing in, basically, to support President Trump and his efforts. Everybody knows that Iran has got to be decimated and destroyed. Think how much better the world would be if we didn't have Iran and all of their sponsored terrorism."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["idioms", "raining cats and dogs", "language origins", "radio broadcast", "live streaming", "podcasts", "gratitude", "positive thinking", "time management", "quality time", "consumer price index", "inflation", "geopolitics", "shipping security", "strait of hormuz", "oil reserves", "g7", "terrorism"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778619042.9778907, "duration_sec": 6.359369516372681, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "webpage_metadata_generation", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "webpage_metadata_generation", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "webpage_markdown", "data": {"window": [[0, "*Please contact us at customerservice@thewarrengroup.com if you have any difficulty logging in. If you are logged in and see a message that says you do not have the correct subscription, your subscription may have lapsed; please renew online or contact us at customerservice@thewarrengroup.com. Please check your spam folder for the password reset link if you have requested it and have not received it.*\n\n## Gain Insight and Intelligence\n\nKeep abreast of significant real estate, banking and financial developments in Massachusetts and Rhode Island. Make smart business decisions with the most comprehensive property and transaction information available anywhere to help you spot trends, generate leads and build strategies.\n\nBecome a **new subscriber** today or log in to access our subscriber-only content.\n\nIf you have any questions regarding your subscription, please call us at 617-896-5367 or email us at subscriptions@thewarrengroup.com."], [0, "CRE Foreclosures Surge 117% Amid Growing Distress - CRE Daily\nMay 7, 2024 \u00b7 Commercial property foreclosures rose a staggering 117% between March 2023 and 2024 , reflecting mounting distress. March 2024 saw 625 commercial foreclosures , the highest number since March 2015 9 years ago. The top foreclosure states in Q1 included California, New York, and Florida, showcasing significant distress across major U.S. regions."], [1, "Real Estate Transactions - Commercial Record\nAccess the largest and most complete real estate database in New England , including sales , price trends, liens and foreclosures . LEARN MORE >>"], [2, "Cap Rate List by US City, State, and Property Type | CapRateIndex\nApr 18, 2026 \u00b7 Cap Rate List Use the Cap Rate List tool to explore average Capitalization Rates ( Cap Rates ) across top U.S. cities and states. The list includes averages by property type Single-Family, Multi-Family , Retail, Office, Industrial, and Specialty for easy comparison. Filter results by state using the dropdown, or search by city, state, or ZIP code."], [3, "U.S. Cap Rate Survey H2 2024 - CBRE\nFeb 12, 2025 \u00b7 The H2 2024 Cap Rate Survey provides a fresh perspective of where market sentiment is trending. Welcome to CBRE\u2019s H2 2024 Cap Rate Survey (CRS). This survey comes at a time when investment sales volume remains muted but investor sentiment has improved. After an annual decline in sales volume of 51% in 2023, 2024 saw an increase of 9%."], [4, "2024 Multifamily Outlook - Freddie Mac\nWhile cap rates have increased over time, gauging the cap rate increase has been difficult due to limited transaction data and sourcing. According to Real Capital Analytics (RCA), cap rates are up 60 bps since the trough in the second quarter of 2022 ; whereas, 10-year Treasury rates have increased twice as much during that time."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": null}, "prompt_chain": null, "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["real estate", "commercial real estate", "multifamily housing", "transaction records", "property data", "property valuation", "market trends", "investment analysis", "capitalization rate", "foreclosure", "liens", "loan default", "interest rates", "treasury yields", "mortgage lending", "market outlook", "sales volume", "credit risk", "united states", "new england"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778619826.2219024, "duration_sec": 3.000575542449951, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And it just gives you one less layer between me and the actual machine itself. I've already got hypervisor, virtual machine, then Docker, then come on, I don't need all those layers. That being said, I think that your intuition is right. I think what you're doing in this maneuver has strengthened your position, has strengthened your trust for me. And I think it just solidifies the fact that Docker is here to stay and containers are the way."], [0, "Yeah, maybe another pithy statement, maybe. Build once runs anywhere, sort of like Docker. Build once runs anywhere, trust always, or something. Trust across the board. And that's true for AI, and then you're right, DHI. It's the same ethos coming through of trust, security is critical across your entire software lifecycle."], [0, "Well, Tushar, you are the head of engineering over there. Anything left to say about what you do, what you're doing, what you're going to do before we close things out? No, maybe I'll just, you know, wrap up, which is, um, I think we're like, you know, sort of where we headed, what are we doing? If it's successful and have high confidence it will be, then like,"], [0, "Everyone who's, one, I think in the entire engineering world, every engine's going to move to using coding agents. It's just what's going to happen. I think all that should run in a new secure runtime for agents using the secure supply chain base that we have. So you use the runtime, basically adapting our engine and our content for the AI world. And I think that is, I think, what everyone needs. And I think we're in a position to do that."], [0, "So I think that's the core focus for us is believe in solve for the world of people using coding agents. But as we do that, I think we're also building the platform that people need for running any agent. So that'll be the next phase once you get through this, that any agent that's running of any kind should run on this layer here."], [0, "That's what we're focused on doing. Honestly, it's like a super, you know, it's really fun. I feel lucky to have this gig. It's a fun gig to be in the middle of all the change in the software world and to be at a place like Docker that's doing this, right? Yeah. It's so critical and so core."], [0, "It's a really fun time to be a developer because there's so much change. It's also quite scary, this change. You know, there's a lot of, there is some uncertainty. While there's so much potential, there's also so much uncertainty and so much pause, but also so much not pause."], [0, "I mean, it's such a conundrum, really, to think about the state of things. But everything is literally changing. We didn't expect this to be where it's at a year ago. A year ago, the conversation was this direction, but not where it's at currently."], [0, "And it's clear that everything from the bottom up is changing about how we develop software, how we deploy software, how we have to secure it, et cetera. It's a fun time because we get to rebuild it all. So if you're an old hat, you're like, okay, that sucks. I don't want to have to learn new stuff. But if you're a new hat, you're like, sweet, let's build some cool stuff. But it is a wild ride we're on right now. I mean, everything is changing."], [0, "It is a wild ride. Also, at least for me, like, you know, look, I struggle to answer the like, hey, where will engineering be and like, where will software development be in two years? I don't know. I can estimate and I can guess a bit. But right now, what's really fun is like, fundamentally, I think people become engineers to like build stuff, like solve problems and innovate. And like coding has been a key way to do that. We just get to like do that at like,"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["docker", "containers", "hypervisor", "virtual machines", "runtime layers", "software development", "engineering", "coding agents", "ai", "secure runtime", "supply chain security", "software lifecycle", "developer experience", "platform development", "automation", "innovation", "uncertainty", "cloud computing"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
