 without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["street promotion", "dental clinics", "pharmacy services", "customer attention", "humor", "comedy", "making people laugh", "sales commissions", "street corner", "appointment free", "immediate service", "territorial competition", "healthcare marketing", "tourism", "patient recruitment", "sales communication", "street economy", "service industry"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778620255.152916, "duration_sec": 2.4117255210876465, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "So we each had a game hen, like a small guy, just three of us. So my wife prepared it one way and I prepared another way and we saw who did the best. That's nice. That was fun. I really like the small turkeys or small game birds for Thanksgiving. Chicken's okay. Turkey's okay. It's not my favorite meat. It tends to get dry quick."], [0, "we would end up with so much extra turkey that's why my mom kind of she'll do like a turkey breast right so we have like turkey you know because it's thanksgiving but that's kind of pivoted to prime rib plus my older sister doesn't like ham and she doesn't really like turkey but she loves steak so she will eat the prime rib um but that sounds like my parents ideal thanksgiving dinner my parents love indian food"], [0, "I mean, my whole family loves Indian food, but my parents love Indian food. So, like, if you have leftovers, feel free to give them to me. My parents will tell you go. I'll bring it to me. We'll just show up. Let me know what day. I'll see if we can figure out a way to keep it till our next recording. Yeah. Perfect. And then we'll go to my wife's parents' place, and they'll have Indian food, too. Mainly Indian food. They won't have too many, like, Western-style food, but their food is going to be super spicy. That's why it depends on the time of day. Hopefully it's for dinner this year, but..."], [0, "It's going to be a fun experience. Yeah. Nice. Cool. Awesome. Romeo, you want to tell us about today's sponsor? Today's sponsor is IMTS Plus, manufacturing digital content to get you ready for IMTS and after. We are hosting videos and articles on topics relevant to manufacturing technologies and the business of manufacturing. It's all free. I guarantee you'll find something you like."], [0, "Awesome, Maria. Thank you. So today, I've got an article on tactile sensors for robots. So end-of-arm tooling and robots have come a long way. And it's fairly interesting that you see evolutions on the three main sections of automation. I break mainly robotic arms to three ways. One is the interface between the robot to what it's interacting with, so the end-of-arm tooling. You have the arm itself. And then you have the, we'll call it brain, that's controlling everything."], [0, "And there's been a lot of evolution on kind of the brain controlling everything, particularly as you connect vision systems or now with cobots, right? So as you add sensors for it to act differently and behave differently, it's fairly interesting. Now they're looking at more sensors at the end of ARM tooling. So as you interact, what they're trying to, in general, either research or industry is headed towards is more dexterity at the end of ARM tooling."], [0, "Not quite till you see like fingers represented, but even like the claws, grips, being able to handle more delicate equipment. And the main use cases that we're seeing is broadening the spectrum of automation. So you look at some of the interesting use cases of like..."], [0, "food separation. So as you're farming food, how do you know what's good and bad? You know, using vision systems to identify what's good and bad, that exists. Use like paddles or compressed air to blow stuff off the conveyors, but shifting to suctions or soft robots or something that can handle a soft"], [0, "semi-ripe food for separating, but also now we're getting the food prep, right? So there's a lot of scenarios where it makes sense to have something delicate. Even in aerospace, like we were just talking about aerodef, aerospace has some really delicate components, electronics, things like that. So shifting towards"], [0, "uh, being, uh, more capable, uh, a piece of automation with, uh, end of arm tooling that has more sensors built into it is where they're headed, but they are headed down an interesting path because the cost of sensors have dropped significantly, but it depends on what tier you're looking at for sensors. So there are quality concerns and, uh, endurance concerns about these sensors because it's constantly being, uh, being used. So it's a double-edged sword when they were looking at this, uh, scenario. So I thought it was really interesting use case, uh, for automation. Uh,"]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["thanksgiving dinner", "game birds", "turkey breast", "prime rib", "indian food", "spicy cuisine", "leftovers", "manufacturing", "digital content", "robotics", "tactile sensors", "end of arm tooling", "agriculture automation", "food processing", "aerospace", "sensor costs", "quality control", "soft robotics"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
